PathwayData.put("data/reading_writing/units/L07U3.json",{"strand":"reading_writing","level":7,"cefr":"B1+","unit":3,"theme":"The Power of Words","essential_question":"How can words change minds and lives?","schema_version":"1.0","core_words":["persuade","opinion","fact","audience","slogan","advertisement","media","headline","bias","source","accurate","rumor","communicate","express"],"recycled_words":["connotation","fact","opinion","purpose","reason","example","formal","paraphrase","cite","first","however","should","evidence"],"rubrics":[{"id":"RW-RUBRIC-5T","title":"Analytic writing rubric","traits":[{"name":"Ideas","name_ar":"الأفكار","bands":[{"score":4,"descriptor":"My ideas are clear, interesting and fully developed with strong details.","descriptor_ar":"أفكاري واضحة وممتعة ومدعومة بتفاصيل قوية."},{"score":3,"descriptor":"My ideas are clear and have enough details.","descriptor_ar":"أفكاري واضحة ومعها تفاصيل كافية."},{"score":2,"descriptor":"My main idea is there but needs more details.","descriptor_ar":"فكرتي الرئيسية موجودة لكنها تحتاج إلى تفاصيل أكثر."},{"score":1,"descriptor":"My ideas are unclear or very few.","descriptor_ar":"أفكاري غير واضحة أو قليلة جدًا."}]},{"name":"Organization","name_ar":"التنظيم","bands":[{"score":4,"descriptor":"Clear beginning, middle and end; ideas flow with linking words.","descriptor_ar":"بداية ووسط ونهاية واضحة، والأفكار مترابطة بكلمات الربط."},{"score":3,"descriptor":"Clear order with some linking words.","descriptor_ar":"ترتيب واضح مع بعض كلمات الربط."},{"score":2,"descriptor":"Some order, but parts are mixed up.","descriptor_ar":"يوجد بعض الترتيب لكن بعض الأجزاء غير مرتبة."},{"score":1,"descriptor":"Ideas are not in order.","descriptor_ar":"الأفكار غير مرتبة."}]},{"name":"Vocabulary","name_ar":"المفردات","bands":[{"score":4,"descriptor":"I use exact, varied words, including new unit words, correctly.","descriptor_ar":"أستخدم كلمات دقيقة ومتنوعة، منها كلمات الوحدة الجديدة، بشكل صحيح."},{"score":3,"descriptor":"I use some new words correctly.","descriptor_ar":"أستخدم بعض الكلمات الجديدة بشكل صحيح."},{"score":2,"descriptor":"I use simple words; some are repeated or wrong.","descriptor_ar":"أستخدم كلمات بسيطة، وبعضها مكرر أو غير صحيح."},{"score":1,"descriptor":"I use very few words; the meaning is hard to understand.","descriptor_ar":"أستخدم كلمات قليلة جدًا ويصعب فهم المعنى."}]},{"name":"Sentence fluency","name_ar":"سلاسة الجمل","bands":[{"score":4,"descriptor":"My sentences are complete, varied in length and easy to read aloud.","descriptor_ar":"جملي كاملة ومتنوعة الطول وسهلة القراءة بصوت عالٍ."},{"score":3,"descriptor":"Most sentences are complete, with some variety.","descriptor_ar":"معظم جملي كاملة مع بعض التنويع."},{"score":2,"descriptor":"Many sentences are short or start the same way.","descriptor_ar":"كثير من جملي قصيرة أو تبدأ بالطريقة نفسها."},{"score":1,"descriptor":"Sentences are incomplete or run together.","descriptor_ar":"جملي ناقصة أو متداخلة."}]},{"name":"Conventions","name_ar":"قواعد الكتابة","bands":[{"score":4,"descriptor":"Capitals, punctuation, spelling and grammar are almost always correct.","descriptor_ar":"الحروف الكبيرة وعلامات الترقيم والإملاء والقواعد صحيحة دائمًا تقريبًا."},{"score":3,"descriptor":"A few mistakes that do not affect meaning.","descriptor_ar":"أخطاء قليلة لا تؤثر في المعنى."},{"score":2,"descriptor":"Several mistakes; the meaning is sometimes unclear.","descriptor_ar":"عدة أخطاء، والمعنى أحيانًا غير واضح."},{"score":1,"descriptor":"Many mistakes make it hard to read.","descriptor_ar":"أخطاء كثيرة تجعل القراءة صعبة."}]}]}],"lessons":[{"id":"RW-L07-U3-LS1","title":"Word Power: The Power of Words","lesson_type":"vocabulary","objectives":["read, say and use 14 words about persuasion and the media and 5 Tier 2 words (convince, manipulate, credible, emphasize, appeal)","identify loaded words – words with strong connotations chosen to influence the reader","replace loaded words with neutral words and explain how persuasive vocabulary (must, clearly, everyone, we) works"],"objectives_ar":["قراءة ونطق واستخدام 14 كلمة عن الإقناع والإعلام و5 كلمات أكاديمية.","تحديد الكلمات المشحونة: كلمات ذات إيحاءات قوية تُختار للتأثير في القارئ.","استبدال الكلمات المشحونة بكلمات محايدة وشرح عمل مفردات الإقناع."],"success_criteria":["I can use the media and persuasion words correctly in sentences.","I can underline loaded words in an advertisement or an argument and say what feeling they create.","I can rewrite a loaded sentence in neutral language."],"success_criteria_ar":["أستطيع استخدام كلمات الإعلام والإقناع في جمل بشكل صحيح.","أستطيع وضع خط تحت الكلمات المشحونة في إعلان أو حجة وذكر الشعور الذي تثيره.","أستطيع إعادة كتابة جملة مشحونة بلغة محايدة."],"map_area":"Vocabulary: Acquisition and Use","vocabulary":[{"word":"persuade","pos":"verb","ipa":"/pərˈsweɪd/","definition":"to make someone agree to do or believe something by giving reasons","arabic":"يُقنع","example":"The advertisement tries to persuade children to buy the cereal.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V01","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V01","tier":"core"},{"word":"opinion","pos":"noun","ipa":"/əˈpɪnjən/","definition":"what a person thinks or believes about something; it cannot be proved","arabic":"رأي","example":"In my opinion, the new rule is fair.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V02","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V02","tier":"core"},{"word":"fact","pos":"noun","ipa":"/fækt/","definition":"something that is true and can be proved","arabic":"حقيقة","example":"It is a fact that Riyadh is the capital of Saudi Arabia.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V03","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V03","tier":"core"},{"word":"audience","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈɔdiəns/","definition":"the people who read, watch or listen to something","arabic":"الجمهور / المتلقّون","example":"The audience for this advertisement is teenagers.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V04","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V04","tier":"core"},{"word":"slogan","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈsloʊɡən/","definition":"a short, easy-to-remember phrase used in advertising or a campaign","arabic":"شعار","example":"The slogan was 'Drink smart, play hard!'","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V05","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V05","tier":"core"},{"word":"advertisement","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌædvərˈtaɪzmənt/","definition":"a notice, picture or video that tries to sell a product or service","arabic":"إعلان","example":"I saw an advertisement for a new phone.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V06","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V06","tier":"core"},{"word":"media","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈmidiə/","definition":"television, radio, newspapers, the internet and other ways of sharing news and information","arabic":"وسائل الإعلام","example":"Social media spreads news very quickly.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V07","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V07","tier":"core"},{"word":"headline","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈhɛdˌlaɪn/","definition":"the title of a news story, printed in large letters","arabic":"عنوان رئيس (للخبر)","example":"The headline said 'Record Rain in Jeddah'.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V08","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V08","tier":"core"},{"word":"bias","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈbaɪəs/","definition":"an unfair preference for or against one side","arabic":"تحيّز","example":"The article showed a clear bias against the new stadium.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V09","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V09","tier":"core"},{"word":"source","pos":"noun","ipa":"/sɔrs/","definition":"the person, book or website where information comes from","arabic":"مصدر","example":"Always check the source of a news story.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V10","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V10","tier":"core"},{"word":"accurate","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈækjərət/","definition":"correct and true in every detail","arabic":"دقيق","example":"The report gave accurate numbers.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V11","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V11","tier":"core"},{"word":"rumor","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈrumər/","definition":"a story that people pass on, which may not be true","arabic":"إشاعة","example":"A rumor spread that school would close.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V12","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V12","tier":"core"},{"word":"communicate","pos":"verb","ipa":"/kəˈmjunəˌkeɪt/","definition":"to share information, ideas or feelings with others","arabic":"يتواصل","example":"Good writers communicate clearly.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V13","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V13","tier":"core"},{"word":"express","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ɪkˈsprɛs/","definition":"to show a feeling or an opinion in words or actions","arabic":"يعبّر عن","example":"She expressed her opinion in a letter.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V14","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V14","tier":"core"},{"word":"convince","pos":"verb","ipa":"/kənˈvɪns/","definition":"to make someone believe that something is true","arabic":"يُقنع (بصحة أمر)","example":"The evidence convinced the judges.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V15","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V15","tier":"tier2"},{"word":"manipulate","pos":"verb","ipa":"/məˈnɪpjəˌleɪt/","definition":"to influence someone unfairly or dishonestly, often without them noticing","arabic":"يتلاعب بـ","example":"Some ads manipulate children's feelings.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V16","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V16","tier":"tier2"},{"word":"credible","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈkrɛdəbəl/","definition":"able to be believed and trusted","arabic":"موثوق / جدير بالتصديق","example":"A doctor is a credible source about health.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V17","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V17","tier":"tier2"},{"word":"emphasize","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ˈɛmfəˌsaɪz/","definition":"to show that something is especially important","arabic":"يؤكّد / يُبرز","example":"The speaker emphasized the danger of rumors.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V18","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V18","tier":"tier2"},{"word":"appeal","pos":"verb / noun","ipa":"/əˈpil/","definition":"to attract or interest someone; an attempt to persuade by using feelings, logic or trust","arabic":"يجذب / استمالة","example":"The ad appeals to children's love of fun.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V19","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V19","tier":"tier2"}],"explanation":{"text":"In Unit 1, you learned that words have **connotations**. **Loaded words** are words with **very strong** positive or negative connotations that writers choose **on purpose** to make readers feel something and **agree** with them. They are common in **advertisements**, **headlines** and **arguments**.\n\nCompare: *a drink with added sugar* (neutral) → *a sweet treat* (positive, loaded) → *a sugar bomb* (negative, loaded). The **facts** are the same, but the **feelings** are very different. Advertisers love positive loaded words: *amazing, natural, new and improved, exclusive, free, limited*. Critics use negative ones: *dangerous, greedy, junk, brainwash, attack*.\n\nOther **persuasive vocabulary**: strong **modal verbs** (*must, should, need to*), **certainty words** (*clearly, obviously, undoubtedly, everyone knows*), **inclusive words** (*we, our, together*) and **absolute words** (*always, never, all, nobody*). Strategy: (1) **Spot** the loaded word. (2) **Ask** what feeling it creates. (3) **Replace** it with a neutral word. (4) **Decide** whether the facts still convince you.","examples":["Neutral: *a snack with a lot of salt* → Loaded (–): ***junk food*** → Loaded (+): ***a tasty treat***.","Neutral: *an inexpensive phone* → Loaded (+): ***an amazing bargain*** → Loaded (–): ***a cheap phone***.","Neutral: *a new rule about phones* → Loaded (–): ***an attack on students' freedom***.","Neutral: *a business that earns money* → Loaded (–): ***a greedy company***.","Advertising slogan: ***New! Improved! 100% natural!*** – three positive loaded words in five words.","Modal verb: ***Parents must act now.*** – sounds urgent.","Certainty word: ***Clearly, this is the best choice.*** – tells readers not to question it.","Inclusive word: ***Together, we can protect our children.*** – makes readers feel part of a team.","Absolute word: ***Everyone is talking about it!*** – is it really everyone?"],"common_errors":["✗ Believing a claim because of exciting words. → ✓ Replace the loaded word and check the facts. (Feelings ≠ facts.)","✗ Thinking *loaded* means *wrong*. → ✓ Loaded words can support a true claim; just notice the effect. (Notice, then judge.)","✗ Using loaded words in a news report. → ✓ News reports should use neutral language. (Know the genre.)","✗ The advertisement persuade children to buy toys. → ✓ The advertisement persuades children to buy toys. (Subject–verb agreement.)","✗ Mixing up *fact* and *opinion*: *It is a fact that pizza is the best food.* → ✓ *In my opinion, pizza is the best food.* (Opinions can't be proved.)","✗ Spelling: *rumour* in American English. → ✓ rumor (American spelling.)","✗ He convinced me to believe the story was true. → ✓ He convinced me that the story was true. / He persuaded me to buy it. (convince + that; persuade + to.)"],"arabic":"تعلّمت في الوحدة الأولى أن للكلمات إيحاءات. والكلمات المشحونة كلمات ذات إيحاءات قوية جدًا، إيجابية أو سلبية، يختارها الكاتب عمدًا ليُشعر القارئ بشيء ويجعله يوافقه، وهي شائعة في الإعلانات والعناوين والحجج.\n\nقارن: مشروب مضاف إليه سكر (محايد)، وحلوى لذيذة (مشحونة إيجابيًا)، وقنبلة سكر (مشحونة سلبيًا). الحقائق واحدة والمشاعر مختلفة. ويحب المعلنون كلمات مثل amazing وnatural وfree، ويستخدم المنتقدون كلمات مثل dangerous وgreedy وjunk.\n\nمن مفردات الإقناع أيضًا: أفعال الوجوب (must, should)، وكلمات اليقين (clearly, obviously)، والكلمات الجامعة (we, our)، والكلمات المطلقة (always, never, everyone). والاستراتيجية: حدّد الكلمة المشحونة، واسأل عن الشعور الذي تثيره، واستبدلها بكلمة محايدة، ثم قرّر هل تقنعك الحقائق.\n\nانتبه: convince + that، وpersuade + to."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:30","narration":"AMAZING! NEW! FREE! Why do some words make us want to buy, click or agree?","on_screen":"The Power of Words 📣","visual":"Flashing advertisement words fly across a screen toward a teenager."},{"time":"00:30–01:30","narration":"Persuade, opinion, fact, audience, slogan, advertisement, media, headline, bias, source, accurate, rumor, communicate, express!","on_screen":"persuade · opinion · fact · audience · slogan · advertisement · media · headline · bias · source · accurate · rumor · communicate · express","visual":"Each word appears on a billboard, a newspaper or a phone screen."},{"time":"01:30–02:10","narration":"Advertisers appeal to our feelings, emphasize the good and try to convince us. Are they credible – or do they manipulate?","on_screen":"convince · manipulate · credible · emphasize · appeal","visual":"Puppet strings above a shopping cart turn into a magnifying glass."},{"time":"02:10–03:30","narration":"A drink with added sugar. A sweet treat. A sugar bomb. Same facts – different feelings. Those are loaded words!","on_screen":"Neutral · Loaded + · Loaded –","visual":"One drink can with three different labels."},{"time":"03:30–04:00","narration":"Spot it, ask what you feel, replace it, then judge the facts!","on_screen":"4 steps to defuse a loaded word","visual":"A word bomb with a fuse is calmly defused."}],"captions_ar":["مذهل! جديد! مجاني! لماذا تجعلنا بعض الكلمات نرغب في الشراء أو النقر أو الموافقة؟","يُقنع، رأي، حقيقة، جمهور، شعار، إعلان، وسائل الإعلام، عنوان رئيس، تحيّز، مصدر، دقيق، إشاعة، يتواصل، يعبّر!","يستميل المعلنون مشاعرنا، ويُبرزون المزايا، ويحاولون إقناعنا. فهل هم موثوقون أم يتلاعبون بنا؟","مشروب مضاف إليه سكر، حلوى لذيذة، قنبلة سكر: الحقائق نفسها والمشاعر مختلفة. هذه هي الكلمات المشحونة!","حدّدها، واسأل عمّا تشعر به، واستبدلها، ثم احكم على الحقائق!"],"voice":"Narrator: American English, lively, slightly playful female voice, speed 1.0."},"texts":[],"activities":[{"id":"RW-L07-U3-LS1-A1","type":"mcq","stage":"controlled","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correct media word.","instructions_ar":"اختر كلمة الإعلام الصحيحة.","items":[{"q":"*Just do your best!* is a famous kind of ___ – a short phrase that is easy to remember.","answer":"slogan","explanation":"Slogan.","explanation_ar":"شعار.","options":["slogan","rumor","source","bias"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"The ___ of the news story was printed in huge letters: *Falcons Return to Taif*.","answer":"headline","explanation":"Headline.","explanation_ar":"العنوان الرئيس.","options":["audience","headline","opinion","media"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"A cartoon channel for young children has a very young ___.","answer":"audience","explanation":"Audience.","explanation_ar":"جمهور.","options":["headline","slogan","fact","audience"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Someone said the school trip was canceled, but it was only a ___.","answer":"rumor","explanation":"Rumor.","explanation_ar":"إشاعة.","options":["fact","source","rumor","slogan"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"The newspaper only interviewed people who supported the plan, so the article showed ___.","answer":"bias","explanation":"Bias.","explanation_ar":"تحيّز.","options":["accuracy","a slogan","an audience","bias"],"dok":2,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"A scientist who studies nutrition is a ___ source of information about healthy eating.","answer":"credible","explanation":"Credible.","explanation_ar":"موثوق.","options":["loaded","rumored","credible","biased"],"dok":2,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"}]},{"id":"RW-L07-U3-LS1-A2","type":"mcq","stage":"guided","instructions":"Guided practice: Loaded words and persuasive vocabulary. Choose.","instructions_ar":"الكلمات المشحونة ومفردات الإقناع: اختر.","items":[{"q":"Which phrase about a fizzy drink uses a NEGATIVE loaded word?","answer":"a sugar bomb","explanation":"Loaded –.","explanation_ar":"مشحونة سلبيًا.","options":["a drink with added sugar","a sweet drink","a fizzy drink","a sugar bomb"],"dok":2,"skill":"loaded words","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which phrase about a new phone uses a POSITIVE loaded word?","answer":"an amazing bargain","explanation":"Loaded +.","explanation_ar":"مشحونة إيجابيًا.","options":["an amazing bargain","an inexpensive phone","a phone that costs less","a cheap phone"],"dok":2,"skill":"loaded words","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which is the BEST neutral replacement for *greedy companies* in a news report?","answer":"companies that want to increase their profits","explanation":"Neutral language.","explanation_ar":"أفضل بديل محايد.","options":["companies that want to increase their profits","evil businesses","wonderful companies","money monsters"],"dok":2,"skill":"loaded words","stage":"guided"},{"q":"In *Everyone is buying the new sneakers!*, which word is an ABSOLUTE word?","answer":"Everyone","explanation":"Absolute words.","explanation_ar":"كلمة مطلقة.","options":["buying","new","Everyone","sneakers"],"dok":2,"skill":"persuasive vocabulary","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which sentence about advertising to children is grammatically correct?","answer":"The commercial persuades children to ask for the toy.","explanation":"persuade + to.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["The commercial persuade children to ask for the toy.","The commercial persuades children to ask for the toy.","The commercial persuades children asking for the toy.","The commercial is persuade children to ask for the toy."],"dok":2,"skill":"grammar","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Why might a writer say *Together, we must protect our children*?","answer":"to make readers feel part of a team and create urgency","explanation":"Inclusive words + modal.","explanation_ar":"لإشعار القارئ بالانتماء وإثارة الإلحاح.","options":["to give an accurate statistic","to make readers feel part of a team and create urgency","to describe a product neutrally","to show the writer's bias against children"],"dok":3,"skill":"persuasive vocabulary","stage":"guided"}]},{"id":"RW-L07-U3-LS1-A3","type":"gap_fill","stage":"freer","instructions":"Freer practice: Use the word bank. Each word fits one sentence.","instructions_ar":"استخدم بنك الكلمات.","items":[{"q":"The bright colors and cartoon heroes ________ to young children.","answer":"appeal","explanation":"Attract.","explanation_ar":"تجذب.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"The speaker ________ that the facts came from three different studies.","answer":"emphasized","explanation":"Showed it was important.","explanation_ar":"أكّد.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"No matter what the ad said, it could not ________ my father that the juice was healthy.","answer":"convince","explanation":"Make believe.","explanation_ar":"يُقنع.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Some advertisers ________ our feelings so that we buy things we do not need.","answer":"manipulate","explanation":"Influence unfairly.","explanation_ar":"يتلاعبون.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Always check whether a website is ________ before you share its news.","answer":"credible","explanation":"Trustworthy.","explanation_ar":"موثوقًا.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"She wrote a letter to the newspaper to ________ her opinion about the new park.","answer":"express","explanation":"Show in words.","explanation_ar":"تعبّر عن.","stage":"freer"}]},{"id":"RW-L07-U3-LS1-A4","type":"open","stage":"production","instructions":"Writing: Write with the new words.","instructions_ar":"اكتب باستخدام الكلمات الجديدة.","items":[{"q":"Describe a school uniform twice: once with positive loaded words and once with negative loaded words.","answer":"Positive: Our smart, elegant uniform makes us look like a proud team. Negative: Our dull, boring uniform makes everyone look the same.","explanation":"Loaded words.","explanation_ar":"الكلمات المشحونة.","dok":2,"skill":"loaded words","stage":"production"},{"q":"Rewrite in neutral language: *This disgusting junk food is destroying our kids!*","answer":"This food contains a lot of fat, sugar or salt and may be unhealthy for children if they eat it often.","explanation":"Neutral.","explanation_ar":"محايد.","dok":2,"skill":"loaded words","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write one fact and one opinion about social media.","answer":"Fact: Many social media apps require users to be at least 13 years old. Opinion: Social media is the best way to keep in touch with friends.","explanation":"Fact vs. opinion.","explanation_ar":"الحقيقة والرأي.","dok":2,"skill":"fact and opinion","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write a slogan for a campaign to stop rumors at school. Explain which persuasive words you used.","answer":"Slogan: 'Check it before you share it – together, we stop rumors!' I used the inclusive word *together* and a short rhythm that is easy to remember.","explanation":"Slogan.","explanation_ar":"الشعار.","dok":3,"skill":"persuasive vocabulary","stage":"production"}]}],"game":{"name":"Defuse the Word Bomb","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["On this screen: answer the lesson questions as fast as you can.","In class: Bomb squad: a sentence appears with a ticking fuse; the child taps the loaded word, chooses the neutral replacement and decides whether the remaining claim is a fact or an opinion before the fuse ends.","A team draws a word bomb card and starts the 30-second timer.","The team names the loaded word(s).","The team replaces each loaded word with a neutral word.","The team decides whether the neutral sentence is a fact or an opinion.","Each step done in time = 1 point; all three = a bonus point."],"data":[]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"Which word means *a story people pass on that may not be true*?","answer":"rumor","explanation":"Rumor.","explanation_ar":"إشاعة.","options":["headline","rumor","source","slogan"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary"},{"q":"What is a *loaded word*?","answer":"a word with a strong connotation chosen to influence the reader","explanation":"Definition.","explanation_ar":"كلمة مشحونة.","options":["a very long word","a word with no meaning","a word with a strong connotation chosen to influence the reader","a word used only in science"],"dok":1,"skill":"loaded words"},{"q":"*The ad tries to **manipulate** children's feelings.* *Manipulate* means ___.","answer":"influence unfairly without them noticing","explanation":"Context.","explanation_ar":"يتلاعب.","options":["describe honestly","protect carefully","influence unfairly without them noticing","ignore completely"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary in context"},{"q":"Which of these is a FACT, not an opinion?","answer":"The headline was printed on the front page.","explanation":"Fact.","explanation_ar":"حقيقة.","options":["The headline was printed on the front page.","This is the most exciting headline ever.","Headlines are boring.","Everyone should love this newspaper."],"dok":1,"skill":"fact and opinion"},{"q":"Which sentence about the rumor and the evidence is correct?","answer":"The evidence convinced me that the rumor was false.","explanation":"convince + that.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["The evidence convinced me that the rumor was false.","The evidence convinced me to the rumor was false.","The evidence convince me that the rumor was false.","The evidence convincing me that the rumor was false."],"dok":2,"skill":"grammar"},{"q":"Which word in *Clearly, this phone is the best choice* is a CERTAINTY word?","answer":"Clearly","explanation":"Certainty words.","explanation_ar":"كلمة يقين.","options":["phone","best","choice","Clearly"],"dok":2,"skill":"persuasive vocabulary"},{"q":"Which is the MOST neutral way to describe an inexpensive car?","answer":"a car with a low price","explanation":"Neutral.","explanation_ar":"الأكثر حيادًا.","options":["an incredible bargain","a car with a low price","a cheap piece of junk","a dream deal"],"dok":2,"skill":"loaded words"},{"q":"Why do advertisers often use words like *new*, *natural* and *free*?","answer":"These words create positive feelings that make products more attractive.","explanation":"Purpose.","explanation_ar":"تثير مشاعر إيجابية.","options":["These words are always accurate facts.","These words are required by law.","These words make ads shorter.","These words create positive feelings that make products more attractive."],"dok":2,"skill":"loaded words"},{"q":"A headline says *Greedy Developers Destroy Beloved Park*. What should a careful reader do FIRST?","answer":"Notice the loaded words and look for the facts behind them.","explanation":"Critical reading.","explanation_ar":"لاحظ الكلمات المشحونة وابحث عن الحقائق.","options":["Share it immediately.","Notice the loaded words and look for the facts behind them.","Believe it because it is a headline.","Ignore all news about parks."],"dok":3,"skill":"loaded words"},{"q":"Why is it important to recognize loaded words in the media?","answer":"so that feelings do not replace facts when we decide what to believe","explanation":"Purpose.","explanation_ar":"كي لا تحل المشاعر محل الحقائق.","options":["so that feelings do not replace facts when we decide what to believe","so that we never read the news","so that we can use more loaded words","because loaded words are always lies"],"dok":3,"skill":"media literacy"}],"images":[{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V01","prompt":"Picture for the word 'persuade': a girl showing a poster to her parents"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V02","prompt":"Picture for the word 'opinion': a speech bubble with a thinking face"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V03","prompt":"Picture for the word 'fact': a check mark next to a globe"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V04","prompt":"Picture for the word 'audience': people watching a screen in a hall"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V05","prompt":"Picture for the word 'slogan': a banner with a catchy phrase"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V06","prompt":"Picture for the word 'advertisement': a billboard showing a smartphone"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V07","prompt":"Picture for the word 'media': icons of a TV, a radio, a newspaper and a phone"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V08","prompt":"Picture for the word 'headline': a newspaper with a big bold title"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V09","prompt":"Picture for the word 'bias': a scale tipped heavily to one side"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V10","prompt":"Picture for the word 'source': a magnifying glass over a website address"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V11","prompt":"Picture for the word 'accurate': a dart in the center of a target"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V12","prompt":"Picture for the word 'rumor': students whispering to each other"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V13","prompt":"Picture for the word 'communicate': two people talking with speech bubbles"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V14","prompt":"Picture for the word 'express': a girl speaking confidently at a microphone"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V15","prompt":"Picture for the word 'convince': a judge nodding after reading a report"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V16","prompt":"Picture for the word 'manipulate': puppet strings above a shopping cart"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V17","prompt":"Picture for the word 'credible': a doctor holding a medical report"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V18","prompt":"Picture for the word 'emphasize': a word underlined twice in red"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V19","prompt":"Picture for the word 'appeal': a bright toy surrounded by stars"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS1-01","prompt":"A teenager surrounded by persuasive words from the media."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS1-02","prompt":"Icons for media words."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS1-03","prompt":"Neutral and loaded labels for the same drink."}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V01","script":"persuade. The advertisement tries to persuade children to buy the cereal."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V02","script":"opinion. In my opinion, the new rule is fair."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V03","script":"fact. It is a fact that Riyadh is the capital of Saudi Arabia."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V04","script":"audience. The audience for this advertisement is teenagers."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V05","script":"slogan. The slogan was 'Drink smart, play hard!'"},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V06","script":"advertisement. I saw an advertisement for a new phone."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V07","script":"media. Social media spreads news very quickly."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V08","script":"headline. The headline said 'Record Rain in Jeddah'."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V09","script":"bias. The article showed a clear bias against the new stadium."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V10","script":"source. Always check the source of a news story."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V11","script":"accurate. The report gave accurate numbers."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V12","script":"rumor. A rumor spread that school would close."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V13","script":"communicate. Good writers communicate clearly."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V14","script":"express. She expressed her opinion in a letter."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V15","script":"convince. The evidence convinced the judges."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V16","script":"manipulate. Some ads manipulate children's feelings."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V17","script":"credible. A doctor is a credible source about health."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V18","script":"emphasize. The speaker emphasized the danger of rumors."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS1-V19","script":"appeal. The ad appeals to children's love of fun."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS1-VID","script":"AMAZING! NEW! FREE! Why do some words make us want to buy, click or agree? Persuade, opinion, fact, audience, slogan, advertisement, media, headline, bias, source, accurate, rumor, communicate, express! Advertisers appeal to our feelings, emphasize the good and try to convince us. Are they credible – or do they manipulate? A drink with added sugar. A sweet treat. A sugar bomb. Same facts – different feelings. Those are loaded words! Spot it, ask what you feel, replace it, then judge the facts!"}]},{"id":"RW-L07-U3-LS2","title":"Advertising to Children Should Be Limited","lesson_type":"close_reading","objectives":["read an opinion article of about 560 words and identify its claim, reasons and evidence","distinguish types of evidence: research, expert organizations, examples and personal stories","explain how the writer answers a counterclaim and uses loaded words to persuade"],"objectives_ar":["قراءة مقالة رأي من نحو 560 كلمة وتحديد ادعائها وأسبابها وأدلتها.","التمييز بين أنواع الأدلة: الأبحاث والمنظمات المتخصصة والأمثلة والقصص الشخصية.","شرح كيف يرد الكاتب على الرأي المخالف ويستخدم الكلمات المشحونة للإقناع."],"success_criteria":["I can state Reem's claim and her three reasons in my own words.","I can name the type of evidence used for each reason.","I can explain the counterclaim and how Reem rebuts it."],"success_criteria_ar":["أستطيع ذكر ادعاء ريم وأسبابها الثلاثة بأسلوبي.","أستطيع تسمية نوع الدليل المستخدم لكل سبب.","أستطيع شرح الرأي المخالف وكيف تردّ عليه ريم."],"map_area":"Informational Text: Key Ideas and Details","vocabulary":[{"word":"commercial","pos":"noun","ipa":"/kəˈmɝʃəl/","definition":"an advertisement on television, radio or the internet","arabic":"إعلان تجاري","example":"The game makes you watch a commercial to get a reward.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS2-V01","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS2-V01","tier":"core"},{"word":"recognize","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ˈrɛkəɡˌnaɪz/","definition":"to know what something is when you see it","arabic":"يميّز / يتعرّف","example":"Young children cannot recognize persuasion.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS2-V02","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS2-V02","tier":"core"},{"word":"marketing","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈmɑrkətɪŋ/","definition":"all the ways a company tries to make people buy its products","arabic":"التسويق","example":"Food marketing influences what children want.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS2-V03","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS2-V03","tier":"core"},{"word":"policy","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈpɑləsi/","definition":"a plan or a set of rules made by a government or an organization","arabic":"سياسة / لائحة","example":"The government introduced a new policy.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS2-V04","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS2-V04","tier":"core"},{"word":"restrict","pos":"verb","ipa":"/rɪˈstrɪkt/","definition":"to limit or control something","arabic":"يقيّد / يحدّ من","example":"Some countries restrict advertising to children.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS2-V05","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS2-V05","tier":"core"},{"word":"promote","pos":"verb","ipa":"/prəˈmoʊt/","definition":"to help sell something by advertising it; to support something","arabic":"يروّج لـ","example":"Healthy foods could still be promoted.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS2-V06","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS2-V06","tier":"core"},{"word":"ban","pos":"noun / verb","ipa":"/bæn/","definition":"an official rule that says something is not allowed; to forbid","arabic":"حظر / يحظر","example":"A limit is not the same as a complete ban.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS2-V07","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS2-V07","tier":"core"}],"explanation":{"text":"An **argument** (opinion article) tries to persuade readers to accept a **claim** – the writer's main opinion (*I believe that advertising aimed at young children should be strictly limited*). The claim is supported by **reasons** (why the writer thinks so), and each reason is supported by **evidence**.\n\nStrong writers use different **types of evidence**: **research** and **statistics** (*a 2004 task force reported that…*), **expert organizations** (*the World Health Organization recommended…*), **real examples** (*Quebec … since 1980*) and **personal stories** (*my seven-year-old brother…*). Ask: *Is the source credible? Is the evidence relevant to the reason?*\n\nFair arguments also present a **counterclaim** – what people on the other side believe – and then a **rebuttal**, which explains why the writer still disagrees (*These points deserve respect. However, a limit is not the same as a complete ban*). Notice the writer's **loaded words** and **point of view**, and decide whether the **evidence**, not just the emotion, convinces you.","examples":["Hook (personal story): ***My seven-year-old brother begged our mother for a new cereal.***","Claim: ***I believe that advertising aimed at young children should be strictly limited.***","Reason 1: ***Young children often cannot tell the difference between an advertisement and a program.***","Evidence – research: ***In 2004, a task force of the American Psychological Association reported…***","Evidence – expert organization: ***In 2023, the World Health Organization recommended strong policies…***","Evidence – real example: ***Sweden does not allow television advertisements aimed at children under twelve.***","Counterclaim: ***Some people argue that limiting advertising is unfair to businesses.***","Rebuttal: ***However, a limit is not the same as a complete ban.***","Loaded / persuasive words: ***strictly, unfair, clever, protect, the youngest members of the audience.***"],"common_errors":["✗ The claim is: advertising. → ✓ The claim is a full opinion sentence: *Advertising to children should be limited.* (Claim = sentence.)","✗ Confusing a reason with evidence. → ✓ A reason says WHY; evidence PROVES the reason. (Reason ≠ evidence.)","✗ Treating the personal story as the strongest evidence. → ✓ Research and expert sources are usually more convincing. (Weigh the evidence.)","✗ Thinking the counterclaim is the writer's opinion. → ✓ The counterclaim is the OTHER side's view. (Read carefully.)","✗ Ignoring the rebuttal. → ✓ Find how the writer answers the other side. (Counterclaim + rebuttal.)","✗ The WHO recommend strong policies in 2023. → ✓ The WHO recommended strong policies in 2023. (Past simple: **recommended**.)","✗ Believing the article because it sounds emotional. → ✓ Check the evidence and the sources. (Logic over feelings.)"],"arabic":"الحجة (مقالة الرأي) تحاول إقناع القارئ بادعاء هو رأي الكاتب الرئيس، ويدعمه بأسباب توضح لماذا يرى ذلك، ويدعم كل سبب بأدلة.\n\nيستخدم الكتّاب الأقوياء أنواعًا مختلفة من الأدلة: الأبحاث والإحصاءات، والمنظمات المتخصصة، والأمثلة الواقعية، والقصص الشخصية. واسأل: هل المصدر موثوق؟ وهل الدليل مرتبط بالسبب؟\n\nوالحجة العادلة تعرض الرأي المخالف ثم تردّ عليه وتشرح لماذا ما زال الكاتب يخالفه. لاحظ الكلمات المشحونة ووجهة نظر الكاتب، وقرّر هل تقنعك الأدلة لا المشاعر فقط.\n\nانتبه: السبب يجيب عن «لماذا؟»، والدليل يثبت السبب."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:30","narration":"A cartoon lion promised my little brother he would be super strong…","on_screen":"Advertising to Children Should Be Limited 📺","visual":"A small boy pointing at a cereal box with a cartoon lion on a screen."},{"time":"00:30–01:40","narration":"Three reasons: children can't recognize persuasion, many ads promote unhealthy food, and online ads are hard to notice.","on_screen":"Claim → 3 reasons","visual":"Three pillars hold up a sign that reads CLAIM."},{"time":"01:40–02:40","narration":"Research, expert organizations, real examples, personal stories – which evidence is strongest?","on_screen":"Types of evidence","visual":"Cards labeled RESEARCH, EXPERT, EXAMPLE, STORY are weighed on a scale."},{"time":"02:40–03:30","narration":"Some people argue… However… That's a counterclaim and a rebuttal!","on_screen":"Counterclaim → Rebuttal","visual":"Two debaters: one speaks, the other answers calmly."},{"time":"03:30–04:00","narration":"Find the claim, check the evidence, notice the loaded words – then decide for yourself.","on_screen":"Read arguments closely","visual":"A magnifying glass moves over the article."}],"captions_ar":["وعد أسدٌ كرتوني أخي الصغير بأنه سيصبح قويًا جدًا…","ثلاثة أسباب: الأطفال لا يميّزون الإقناع، وكثير من الإعلانات يروّج لطعام غير صحي، والإعلانات الإلكترونية يصعب ملاحظتها.","أبحاث، ومنظمات متخصصة، وأمثلة واقعية، وقصص شخصية: أي الأدلة أقوى؟","يرى بعض الناس… لكن… هذا رأي مخالف وردّ عليه!","حدّد الادعاء، وافحص الأدلة، ولاحظ الكلمات المشحونة، ثم قرّر بنفسك."],"voice":"Narrator: American English, confident teenage female voice for Reem; clear teacher voice for the explanation; speed 1.0."},"texts":[{"type":"reading","title":"Advertising to Children Should Be Limited","body":"*An opinion article by Reem Al-Shehri, Grade 9, for the school magazine*\n\nLast week, my seven-year-old brother begged our mother for a new cereal. He did not know what was in it, and he did not care. He wanted it because a cartoon lion on a screen had promised that it would make him \"super strong.\" My brother is not unusual. Every day, children are surrounded by advertisements on television, on video sites and even inside the games they play. I believe that advertising aimed at young children should be strictly limited.\n\n**Young children cannot recognize persuasion** – The first reason is that young children often cannot tell the difference between an advertisement and a program. In 2004, a task force of the American Psychological Association reported that most children under about eight years old do not understand that advertisements are designed to persuade them. To a young child, the cartoon lion is simply a friend giving good advice. It is unfair to use clever persuasive techniques on an audience that cannot recognize them.\n\n**Many advertisements promote unhealthy food** – Second, many of the advertisements that children see are for foods high in sugar, fat or salt. The World Health Organization has warned that this kind of marketing influences what children want to eat, and in 2023 it recommended that governments introduce strong policies to protect children from it. When children eat these foods too often, they are more likely to develop health problems such as tooth decay and unhealthy weight gain. A bright, funny commercial for a sugary drink is far more exciting to a child than a quiet message about vegetables, so the competition is not fair.\n\n**Online advertising is harder to notice** – Third, advertising has moved online, where it is even harder to recognize. In some children's games, a character offers a \"special reward\" if the player buys an item or watches a commercial. Some videos made by popular online stars are paid for by companies, but young viewers may not realize that their favorite video is really an advertisement. Some apps also collect information about what children watch so that they can show them even more advertisements. Parents cannot watch every screen at every moment.\n\n**What about the other side?** – Some people argue that limiting advertising is unfair to businesses, which need to tell customers about their products. Others say that parents, not governments, should decide what children watch, or that advertisements help to pay for free programs and apps. These points deserve respect. However, a limit is not the same as a complete ban. Companies could still advertise to parents, and healthy products could still be promoted. Free entertainment should not come at the cost of children's health. Some places have already acted. Quebec, in Canada, has restricted commercial advertising directed at children under thirteen since 1980, and Sweden does not allow television advertisements aimed at children under twelve.\n\nChildren deserve to grow up making choices based on facts, not on the promises of a cartoon lion. By limiting advertising aimed at young children, we can give families more control and help children build healthy habits. Governments could set clear rules for children's channels and apps, companies could choose to advertise healthier products, and schools could teach students how to recognize persuasion. It is time for governments, companies and parents to work together to protect the youngest members of the audience.","word_count":564,"audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS2-T1","id":"TXT-RW-L07-U3-LS2-1","genre":"opinion/argument","role":"main","difficulty":"800L–950L","glossary":[{"word":"task force","definition":"a group of experts formed to study one problem","arabic":"فريق عمل / لجنة مختصة"},{"word":"commercial","definition":"an advertisement on television, radio or the internet","arabic":"إعلان تجاري"},{"word":"tooth decay","definition":"damage to teeth caused by bacteria and sugar","arabic":"تسوّس الأسنان"}]}],"activities":[{"id":"RW-L07-U3-LS2-A1","type":"mcq","stage":"controlled","instructions":"Controlled practice: Read the article. Choose the answer.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ المقالة واختر الإجابة.","items":[{"q":"Why did Reem's brother want the new cereal?","answer":"A cartoon lion promised it would make him super strong.","explanation":"Paragraph 2.","explanation_ar":"وعده أسد كرتوني بالقوة.","options":["He knew it was healthy.","His friends ate it every day.","It was on sale.","A cartoon lion promised it would make him super strong."],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Which organization's task force reported on children and advertising in 2004?","answer":"the American Psychological Association","explanation":"Reason 1.","explanation_ar":"الجمعية الأمريكية لعلم النفس.","options":["the World Health Organization","the school magazine","the American Psychological Association","a cereal company"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Under about what age do most children NOT understand that ads are designed to persuade, according to the task force?","answer":"eight","explanation":"Reason 1.","explanation_ar":"نحو ثماني سنوات.","options":["thirteen","eight","twelve","five"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What did the World Health Organization recommend in 2023?","answer":"strong government policies to protect children from unhealthy food marketing","explanation":"Reason 2.","explanation_ar":"سياسات حكومية قوية.","options":["a ban on all cereal","more cartoon advertisements","strong government policies to protect children from unhealthy food marketing","longer school days"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What does a character in some children's games offer, according to Reem?","answer":"a special reward if the player buys an item or watches a commercial","explanation":"Reason 3.","explanation_ar":"مكافأة خاصة.","options":["a special reward if the player buys an item or watches a commercial","free healthy food","a lesson about persuasion","a trip to Sweden"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Since when has Quebec restricted commercial advertising directed at children under thirteen?","answer":"since 1980","explanation":"Counterclaim paragraph.","explanation_ar":"منذ 1980.","options":["since 2004","since 2023","since 1991","since 1980"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"}]},{"id":"RW-L07-U3-LS2-A2","type":"mcq","stage":"guided","instructions":"Guided practice: Claim, evidence and counterclaim. Choose.","instructions_ar":"الادعاء والدليل والرأي المخالف: اختر.","items":[{"q":"Which sentence states Reem's CLAIM?","answer":"I believe that advertising aimed at young children should be strictly limited.","explanation":"Claim.","explanation_ar":"الادعاء.","options":["My brother is not unusual.","I believe that advertising aimed at young children should be strictly limited.","Some apps also collect information about what children watch so that they can show them even more advertisements. Parents cannot watch every screen at every moment.","These points deserve respect."],"dok":2,"skill":"argument structure","stage":"guided"},{"q":"What TYPE of evidence is *Quebec … has restricted commercial advertising directed at children under thirteen since 1980*?","answer":"a real-world example","explanation":"Evidence type.","explanation_ar":"مثال واقعي.","options":["a personal story","an opinion","a slogan","a real-world example"],"dok":2,"skill":"evidence","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which sentence is a COUNTERCLAIM in Reem's article?","answer":"Some people argue that limiting advertising is unfair to businesses.","explanation":"Counterclaim.","explanation_ar":"الرأي المخالف.","options":["Advertising has moved online.","Many advertisements are for foods high in sugar.","Some people argue that limiting advertising is unfair to businesses.","Children deserve to grow up making choices based on facts."],"dok":2,"skill":"argument structure","stage":"guided"},{"q":"How does Reem REBUT the counterclaim?","answer":"She explains that a limit is not a complete ban, so companies can still advertise to parents.","explanation":"Rebuttal.","explanation_ar":"الرد.","options":["She explains that a limit is not a complete ban, so companies can still advertise to parents.","She says businesses are always greedy.","She agrees completely and changes her claim.","She ignores it."],"dok":2,"skill":"argument structure","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Why does Reem begin with the story about her brother?","answer":"to hook readers with a real, relatable example","explanation":"Author's craft.","explanation_ar":"لجذب القارئ بمثال واقعي.","options":["to give the strongest scientific evidence","to state the counterclaim","to hook readers with a real, relatable example","to describe a cereal recipe"],"dok":3,"skill":"author's craft","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which phrase is a LOADED expression that appeals to the reader's feelings?","answer":"the youngest members of the audience","explanation":"Loaded words.","explanation_ar":"عبارة مشحونة.","options":["in 2023","the youngest members of the audience","foods high in sugar, fat or salt","children under thirteen"],"dok":3,"skill":"loaded words","stage":"guided"}]},{"id":"RW-L07-U3-LS2-A3","type":"highlight","stage":"freer","instructions":"Find the evidence: Find the evidence.","instructions_ar":"ابحث عن الدليل.","items":[{"q":"Underline the sentence that explains why it is unfair to advertise to young children.","answer":"It is unfair to use clever persuasive techniques on an audience that cannot recognize them.","explanation":"Reason 1.","explanation_ar":"السبب الأول.","evidence":"It is unfair to use clever persuasive techniques on an audience that cannot recognize them.","text_id":"TXT-RW-L07-U3-LS2-1","dok":2,"skill":"argument structure","stage":"freer","sentences":["*An opinion article by Reem Al-Shehri, Grade 9, for the school magazine*","Last week, my seven-year-old brother begged our mother for a new cereal.","He did not know what was in it, and he did not care.","He wanted it because a cartoon lion on a screen had promised that it would make him \"super strong.\"","My brother is not unusual.","Every day, children are surrounded by advertisements on television, on video sites and even inside the games they play.","I believe that advertising aimed at young children should be strictly limited.","**Young children cannot recognize persuasion** – The first reason is that young children often cannot tell the difference between an advertisement and a program.","In 2004, a task force of the American Psychological Association reported that most children under about eight years old do not understand that advertisements are designed to persuade them.","To a young child, the cartoon lion is simply a friend giving good advice.","It is unfair to use clever persuasive techniques on an audience that cannot recognize them.","**Many advertisements promote unhealthy food** – Second, many of the advertisements that children see are for foods high in sugar, fat or salt.","The World Health Organization has warned that this kind of marketing influences what children want to eat, and in 2023 it recommended that governments introduce strong policies to protect children from it.","When children eat these foods too often, they are more likely to develop health problems such as tooth decay and unhealthy weight gain.","A bright, funny commercial for a sugary drink is far more exciting to a child than a quiet message about vegetables, so the competition is not fair.","**Online advertising is harder to notice** – Third, advertising has moved online, where it is even harder to recognize.","In some children's games, a character offers a \"special reward\" if the player buys an item or watches a commercial.","Some videos made by popular online stars are paid for by companies, but young viewers may not realize that their favorite video is really an advertisement.","Some apps also collect information about what children watch so that they can show them even more advertisements.","Parents cannot watch every screen at every moment.","**What about the other side?** – Some people argue that limiting advertising is unfair to businesses, which need to tell customers about their products.","Others say that parents, not governments, should decide what children watch, or that advertisements help to pay for free programs and apps.","These points deserve respect.","However, a limit is not the same as a complete ban.","Companies could still advertise to parents, and healthy products could still be promoted.","Free entertainment should not come at the cost of children's health.","Some places have already acted.","Quebec, in Canada, has restricted commercial advertising directed at children under thirteen since 1980, and Sweden does not allow television advertisements aimed at children under twelve.","Children deserve to grow up making choices based on facts, not on the promises of a cartoon lion.","By limiting advertising aimed at young children, we can give families more control and help children build healthy habits.","Governments could set clear rules for children's channels and apps, companies could choose to advertise healthier products, and schools could teach students how to recognize persuasion.","It is time for governments, companies and parents to work together to protect the youngest members of the audience."]},{"q":"Underline the sentence where Reem shows respect for the other side.","answer":"These points deserve respect.","explanation":"Counterclaim.","explanation_ar":"الرأي المخالف.","evidence":"These points deserve respect.","text_id":"TXT-RW-L07-U3-LS2-1","dok":2,"skill":"argument structure","stage":"freer","sentences":["*An opinion article by Reem Al-Shehri, Grade 9, for the school magazine*","Last week, my seven-year-old brother begged our mother for a new cereal.","He did not know what was in it, and he did not care.","He wanted it because a cartoon lion on a screen had promised that it would make him \"super strong.\"","My brother is not unusual.","Every day, children are surrounded by advertisements on television, on video sites and even inside the games they play.","I believe that advertising aimed at young children should be strictly limited.","**Young children cannot recognize persuasion** – The first reason is that young children often cannot tell the difference between an advertisement and a program.","In 2004, a task force of the American Psychological Association reported that most children under about eight years old do not understand that advertisements are designed to persuade them.","To a young child, the cartoon lion is simply a friend giving good advice.","It is unfair to use clever persuasive techniques on an audience that cannot recognize them.","**Many advertisements promote unhealthy food** – Second, many of the advertisements that children see are for foods high in sugar, fat or salt.","The World Health Organization has warned that this kind of marketing influences what children want to eat, and in 2023 it recommended that governments introduce strong policies to protect children from it.","When children eat these foods too often, they are more likely to develop health problems such as tooth decay and unhealthy weight gain.","A bright, funny commercial for a sugary drink is far more exciting to a child than a quiet message about vegetables, so the competition is not fair.","**Online advertising is harder to notice** – Third, advertising has moved online, where it is even harder to recognize.","In some children's games, a character offers a \"special reward\" if the player buys an item or watches a commercial.","Some videos made by popular online stars are paid for by companies, but young viewers may not realize that their favorite video is really an advertisement.","Some apps also collect information about what children watch so that they can show them even more advertisements.","Parents cannot watch every screen at every moment.","**What about the other side?** – Some people argue that limiting advertising is unfair to businesses, which need to tell customers about their products.","Others say that parents, not governments, should decide what children watch, or that advertisements help to pay for free programs and apps.","These points deserve respect.","However, a limit is not the same as a complete ban.","Companies could still advertise to parents, and healthy products could still be promoted.","Free entertainment should not come at the cost of children's health.","Some places have already acted.","Quebec, in Canada, has restricted commercial advertising directed at children under thirteen since 1980, and Sweden does not allow television advertisements aimed at children under twelve.","Children deserve to grow up making choices based on facts, not on the promises of a cartoon lion.","By limiting advertising aimed at young children, we can give families more control and help children build healthy habits.","Governments could set clear rules for children's channels and apps, companies could choose to advertise healthier products, and schools could teach students how to recognize persuasion.","It is time for governments, companies and parents to work together to protect the youngest members of the audience."]},{"q":"Why does Reem mention that some videos are paid for by companies?","answer":"to show that children may not realize a video is really an advertisement","explanation":"Reason 3.","explanation_ar":"لإظهار أن الطفل قد لا يدرك أنه إعلان.","options":["to show that children may not realize a video is really an advertisement","to recommend her favorite videos","to prove that all videos are bad","to explain how to become an online star"],"dok":2,"skill":"evidence","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Which piece of evidence is MOST credible for Reason 2?","answer":"the World Health Organization's 2023 recommendation","explanation":"Credible sources.","explanation_ar":"توصية منظمة الصحة العالمية.","options":["the story about Reem's brother","the cartoon lion's promise","Reem's opinion about cereal","the World Health Organization's 2023 recommendation"],"dok":3,"skill":"evaluating evidence","stage":"freer"}]},{"id":"RW-L07-U3-LS2-A4","type":"open","stage":"production","instructions":"Writing: Analyze the argument.","instructions_ar":"حلّل الحجة.","items":[{"q":"Write Reem's claim and her three reasons in your own words.","answer":"Claim: ads for young children should be limited. Reasons: young children don't understand ads, many ads sell unhealthy food, and online ads are hidden in games and videos.","explanation":"Claim + reasons.","explanation_ar":"الادعاء والأسباب.","dok":2,"skill":"argument structure","stage":"production"},{"q":"Choose one reason. Explain how the evidence supports it.","answer":"Reason 1 says young children can't recognize persuasion. The 2004 task force found that most children under about eight don't understand that ads try to persuade them, which supports the idea that advertising to them is unfair.","explanation":"Evidence.","explanation_ar":"الدليل.","dok":3,"skill":"evidence","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write the counterclaim and Reem's rebuttal in your own words.","answer":"Counterclaim: limits hurt businesses and parents should decide. Rebuttal: limits are not a total ban, companies can still talk to parents, and some places already have such rules.","explanation":"Counterclaim + rebuttal.","explanation_ar":"الرأي المخالف والرد.","dok":2,"skill":"argument structure","stage":"production"},{"q":"Do you agree with Reem? Give one reason with evidence from the text or your own experience.","answer":"I agree, because my little cousin also wants toys only because of ads, and the 2004 task force showed that young children can't recognize persuasion.","explanation":"Respond.","explanation_ar":"استجب.","dok":3,"skill":"evaluating arguments","stage":"production"}]},{"id":"RW-L07-U3-LS2-TR-1","type":"timed_reading","instructions":"Timed reading: Read \"Advertising to Children Should Be Limited\" aloud or silently. Try to finish in the time.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ النص وحاول أن تنهيه في الوقت المحدد.","items":[{"q":"Read “Advertising to Children Should Be Limited”.","answer":"564 words","title":"Advertising to Children Should Be Limited","body":"*An opinion article by Reem Al-Shehri, Grade 9, for the school magazine*\n\nLast week, my seven-year-old brother begged our mother for a new cereal. He did not know what was in it, and he did not care. He wanted it because a cartoon lion on a screen had promised that it would make him \"super strong.\" My brother is not unusual. Every day, children are surrounded by advertisements on television, on video sites and even inside the games they play. I believe that advertising aimed at young children should be strictly limited.\n\n**Young children cannot recognize persuasion** – The first reason is that young children often cannot tell the difference between an advertisement and a program. In 2004, a task force of the American Psychological Association reported that most children under about eight years old do not understand that advertisements are designed to persuade them. To a young child, the cartoon lion is simply a friend giving good advice. It is unfair to use clever persuasive techniques on an audience that cannot recognize them.\n\n**Many advertisements promote unhealthy food** – Second, many of the advertisements that children see are for foods high in sugar, fat or salt. The World Health Organization has warned that this kind of marketing influences what children want to eat, and in 2023 it recommended that governments introduce strong policies to protect children from it. When children eat these foods too often, they are more likely to develop health problems such as tooth decay and unhealthy weight gain. A bright, funny commercial for a sugary drink is far more exciting to a child than a quiet message about vegetables, so the competition is not fair.\n\n**Online advertising is harder to notice** – Third, advertising has moved online, where it is even harder to recognize. In some children's games, a character offers a \"special reward\" if the player buys an item or watches a commercial. Some videos made by popular online stars are paid for by companies, but young viewers may not realize that their favorite video is really an advertisement. Some apps also collect information about what children watch so that they can show them even more advertisements. Parents cannot watch every screen at every moment.\n\n**What about the other side?** – Some people argue that limiting advertising is unfair to businesses, which need to tell customers about their products. Others say that parents, not governments, should decide what children watch, or that advertisements help to pay for free programs and apps. These points deserve respect. However, a limit is not the same as a complete ban. Companies could still advertise to parents, and healthy products could still be promoted. Free entertainment should not come at the cost of children's health. Some places have already acted. Quebec, in Canada, has restricted commercial advertising directed at children under thirteen since 1980, and Sweden does not allow television advertisements aimed at children under twelve.\n\nChildren deserve to grow up making choices based on facts, not on the promises of a cartoon lion. By limiting advertising aimed at young children, we can give families more control and help children build healthy habits. Governments could set clear rules for children's channels and apps, companies could choose to advertise healthier products, and schools could teach students how to recognize persuasion. It is time for governments, companies and parents to work together to protect the youngest members of the audience.","words":564,"limit":275}],"time_limit_sec":275},{"id":"RW-L07-U3-LS2-ORG","type":"organizer","organizer":"Argument Map","instructions":"Organizer: Map Reem's argument. Write the claim, the three reasons with their evidence, the counterclaim and the rebuttal.","items":[{"q":"Claim","answer":"Advertising aimed at young children should be strictly limited."},{"q":"Reason 1 + evidence","answer":"Young children can't recognize persuasion – 2004 American Psychological Association task force (under about 8)"},{"q":"Reason 2 + evidence","answer":"Many ads promote unhealthy food – World Health Organization 2023 recommendation; health problems"},{"q":"Reason 3 + evidence","answer":"Online ads are harder to notice – game rewards, paid videos; parents can't watch every screen (examples)"},{"q":"Counterclaim","answer":"Limits are unfair to businesses; parents, not governments, should decide."},{"q":"Rebuttal","answer":"A limit is not a ban; companies can still advertise to parents and promote healthy products; Quebec and Sweden already restrict ads."}]}],"game":{"name":"Argument Architect","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["On this screen: answer the lesson questions as fast as you can.","In class: Argument builder: the child drags sentences onto the roof, pillars, bricks, side door and lock; correct placements make the house stand. Weak evidence bricks (opinions) crack and must be replaced.","Teams receive mixed sentence strips for one argument.","They place each strip on the correct part of the house.","They test each evidence brick: is it research, an expert, an example or a story?","They read the finished argument aloud.","Correct placement = 1 point; correct evidence type = 1 point; a standing house = 3 bonus points."],"data":[]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"In an argument, what is the *claim*?","answer":"the writer's main opinion that he or she wants readers to accept","explanation":"Definition.","explanation_ar":"الادعاء.","options":["the writer's main opinion that he or she wants readers to accept","a piece of evidence","the title of the article","the other side's view"],"dok":1,"skill":"argument structure"},{"q":"What is a *commercial*?","answer":"an advertisement on television, radio or the internet","explanation":"Vocabulary.","explanation_ar":"إعلان تجاري.","options":["a cereal box","a school magazine","a government rule","an advertisement on television, radio or the internet"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary"},{"q":"*Some countries **restrict** advertising to children.* *Restrict* means ___.","answer":"limit or control","explanation":"Context.","explanation_ar":"تقيّد.","options":["encourage","pay for","limit or control","invent"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary in context"},{"q":"What is a *counterclaim*?","answer":"the opposing view that some people hold","explanation":"Definition.","explanation_ar":"الرأي المخالف.","options":["the writer's strongest evidence","the opposing view that some people hold","the conclusion","a loaded word"],"dok":1,"skill":"argument structure"},{"q":"Which sentence about the World Health Organization is correct?","answer":"In 2023, the WHO recommended strong policies.","explanation":"Past simple.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["In 2023, the WHO recommend strong policies.","In 2023, the WHO recommending strong policies.","In 2023, the WHO recommended strong policies.","In 2023, the WHO has recommend strong policies."],"dok":2,"skill":"grammar"},{"q":"What is the difference between a reason and evidence?","answer":"A reason explains why; evidence proves that the reason is true.","explanation":"Reason vs. evidence.","explanation_ar":"السبب يفسّر والدليل يثبت.","options":["They are exactly the same.","A reason explains why; evidence proves that the reason is true.","Evidence is always an opinion.","A reason is always a statistic."],"dok":2,"skill":"argument structure"},{"q":"What is Reem's point of view about advertising aimed at young children?","answer":"She thinks it should be strictly limited to protect them.","explanation":"Point of view.","explanation_ar":"يجب تقييده.","options":["She thinks it should be strictly limited to protect them.","She thinks it should be increased.","She has no opinion.","She thinks only parents should advertise."],"dok":2,"skill":"point of view"},{"q":"Why does including a counterclaim make Reem's argument stronger?","answer":"It shows she has considered the other side and can answer it.","explanation":"Purpose.","explanation_ar":"يُظهر أنها درست الرأي الآخر.","options":["It makes the article longer.","It shows she has considered the other side and can answer it.","It proves she is wrong.","It replaces the need for evidence."],"dok":2,"skill":"argument structure"},{"q":"Which addition would make Reem's argument MORE credible?","answer":"a recent study from a university about children and online ads","explanation":"Evaluate.","explanation_ar":"دراسة حديثة من جامعة.","options":["a joke about cartoon lions","a list of her favorite cereals","more exclamation marks","a recent study from a university about children and online ads"],"dok":3,"skill":"evaluating evidence"},{"q":"Which question should a critical reader ask about Reem's article?","answer":"Is the evidence credible and relevant, or am I persuaded only by feelings?","explanation":"Critical reading.","explanation_ar":"سؤال القارئ الناقد.","options":["How many words are in the title?","What color is the cereal box?","Is Reem older than her brother?","Is the evidence credible and relevant, or am I persuaded only by feelings?"],"dok":3,"skill":"evaluating arguments"}],"images":[{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS2-V01","prompt":"Picture for the word 'commercial': a TV screen showing an advertisement"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS2-V02","prompt":"Picture for the word 'recognize': a child pointing at a familiar face"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS2-V03","prompt":"Picture for the word 'marketing': a poster, a phone ad and a toy in a cereal box"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS2-V04","prompt":"Picture for the word 'policy': an official document with a stamp"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS2-V05","prompt":"Picture for the word 'restrict': a gate partly closed across a road"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS2-V06","prompt":"Picture for the word 'promote': a poster advertising fruit"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS2-V07","prompt":"Picture for the word 'ban': a red circle with a line through it"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS2-01","prompt":"A child influenced by a cartoon advertisement."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS2-02","prompt":"Hidden advertising in children's games and videos."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS2-03","prompt":"The parts of an argument shown as a house."}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS2-V01","script":"commercial. The game makes you watch a commercial to get a reward."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS2-V02","script":"recognize. Young children cannot recognize persuasion."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS2-V03","script":"marketing. Food marketing influences what children want."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS2-V04","script":"policy. The government introduced a new policy."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS2-V05","script":"restrict. Some countries restrict advertising to children."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS2-V06","script":"promote. Healthy foods could still be promoted."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS2-V07","script":"ban. A limit is not the same as a complete ban."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS2-T1","script":"*An opinion article by Reem Al-Shehri, Grade 9, for the school magazine* Last week, my seven-year-old brother begged our mother for a new cereal. He did not know what was in it, and he did not care. He wanted it because a cartoon lion on a screen had promised that it would make him \"super strong.\" My brother is not unusual. Every day, children are surrounded by advertisements on television, on video sites and even inside the games they play. I believe that advertising aimed at young children should be strictly limited. **Young children cannot recognize persuasion** – The first reason is that young children often cannot tell the difference between an advertisement and a program. In 2004, a task force of the American Psychological Association reported that most children under about eight years old do not understand that advertisements are designed to persuade them. To a young child, the cartoon lion is simply a friend giving good advice. It is unfair to use clever persuasive techniques on an audience that cannot recognize them. **Many advertisements promote unhealthy food** – Second, many of the advertisements that children see are for foods high in sugar, fat or salt. The World Health Organization has warned that this kind of marketing influences what children want to eat, and in 2023 it recommended that governments introduce strong policies to protect children from it. When children eat these foods too often, they are more likely to develop health problems such as tooth decay and unhealthy weight gain. A bright, funny commercial for a sugary drink is far more exciting to a child than a quiet message about vegetables, so the competition is not fair. **Online advertising is harder to notice** – Third, advertising has moved online, where it is even harder to recognize. In some children's games, a character offers a \"special reward\" if the player buys an item or watches a commercial. Some videos made by popular online stars are paid for by companies, but young viewers may not realize that their favorite video is really an advertisement. Some apps also collect information about what children watch so that they can show them even more advertisements. Parents cannot watch every screen at every moment. **What about the other side?** – Some people argue that limiting advertising is unfair to businesses, which need to tell customers about their products. Others say that parents, not governments, should decide what children watch, or that advertisements help to pay for free programs and apps. These points deserve respect. However, a limit is not the same as a complete ban. Companies could still advertise to parents, and healthy products could still be promoted. Free entertainment should not come at the cost of children's health. Some places have already acted. Quebec, in Canada, has restricted commercial advertising directed at children under thirteen since 1980, and Sweden does not allow television advertisements aimed at children under twelve. Children deserve to grow up making choices based on facts, not on the promises of a cartoon lion. By limiting advertising aimed at young children, we can give families more control and help children build healthy habits. Governments could set clear rules for children's channels and apps, companies could choose to advertise healthier products, and schools could teach students how to recognize persuasion. It is time for governments, companies and parents to work together to protect the youngest members of the audience."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS2-VID","script":"A cartoon lion promised my little brother he would be super strong… Three reasons: children can't recognize persuasion, many ads promote unhealthy food, and online ads are hard to notice. Research, expert organizations, real examples, personal stories – which evidence is strongest? Some people argue… However… That's a counterclaim and a rebuttal! Find the claim, check the evidence, notice the loaded words – then decide for yourself."}]},{"id":"RW-L07-U3-LS3","title":"Point of view and persuasive techniques: \"How Headlines Hook Readers\"","lesson_type":"strategy_paired_text","objectives":["identify an author's point of view and purpose in an argument and an informational text","recognize persuasive techniques: appeals to logic, emotion and trust, rhetorical questions, bandwagon, testimonials and loaded words","compare how *Advertising to Children Should Be Limited* and *How Headlines Hook Readers* treat persuasion"],"objectives_ar":["تحديد وجهة نظر الكاتب وغرضه في نص حجاجي ونص معلوماتي.","التعرف على أساليب الإقناع: استمالة العقل والعاطفة والثقة، والأسئلة البلاغية، واتباع الجماعة، والشهادات، والكلمات المشحونة.","مقارنة تناول النصين لموضوع الإقناع."],"success_criteria":["I can state each author's point of view and purpose with evidence.","I can name the technique in a headline or a sentence and explain its effect.","I can explain one way the two texts are similar and one way they are different."],"success_criteria_ar":["أستطيع ذكر وجهة نظر كل كاتب وغرضه بالدليل.","أستطيع تسمية الأسلوب في عنوان أو جملة وشرح أثره.","أستطيع شرح وجه شبه ووجه اختلاف بين النصين."],"map_area":"Informational Text: Language, Craft, and Structure","vocabulary":[{"word":"clickbait","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈklɪkˌbeɪt/","definition":"a headline or picture online that is designed to make you click, often by exaggerating","arabic":"طُعم النقرات","example":"That headline is clickbait – the article says almost nothing.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS3-V01","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS3-V01","tier":"core"},{"word":"misleading","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/mɪsˈlidɪŋ/","definition":"giving a wrong idea or impression","arabic":"مضلِّل","example":"The headline was misleading.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS3-V02","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS3-V02","tier":"core"},{"word":"technique","pos":"noun","ipa":"/tɛkˈnik/","definition":"a particular way of doing something","arabic":"أسلوب / تقنية","example":"A question is a common headline technique.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS3-V03","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS3-V03","tier":"core"},{"word":"curiosity","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌkjʊriˈɑsəti/","definition":"a strong wish to know or learn something","arabic":"فضول / حب الاستطلاع","example":"The headline made me click out of curiosity.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS3-V04","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS3-V04","tier":"core"},{"word":"bandwagon","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈbændˌwæɡən/","definition":"a technique that says you should do something because everyone else is doing it","arabic":"اتباع الجماعة (أسلوب إقناع)","example":"'Join millions of happy users!' is a bandwagon appeal.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS3-V05","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS3-V05","tier":"core"},{"word":"testimonial","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌtɛstəˈmoʊniəl/","definition":"a statement by a person, often famous, saying that a product is good","arabic":"شهادة / تزكية","example":"The ad uses a testimonial from a famous athlete.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS3-V06","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS3-V06","tier":"core"},{"word":"rhetorical question","pos":"noun","ipa":"/rɪˈtɔrɪkəl ˈkwɛstʃən/","definition":"a question asked to make a point, not to get an answer","arabic":"سؤال بلاغي","example":"'Don't our children deserve better?' is a rhetorical question.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS3-V07","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS3-V07","tier":"core"}],"explanation":{"text":"Every author has a **point of view** – what he or she **believes** about the topic – and a **purpose**: to **inform**, to **persuade** or to **entertain**. Find the point of view by looking at the **claim**, the **evidence the author chooses** and the **words** he or she uses. Reem believes advertising to children should be limited (purpose: **persuade**). The headline article explains how headlines work and warns readers to be careful (purposes: **inform** and **advise**).\n\nWriters use three main **appeals**: **logic** – facts, statistics and reasons (*a 2004 task force reported…*); **emotion** – stories and feelings (*my seven-year-old brother begged…*); **trust/authority** – experts and credible sources (*the World Health Organization recommended…*).\n\nOther common **techniques**: **rhetorical questions** (*Don't our children deserve better?*), **bandwagon** (*Everyone is joining!*), **testimonials** (*A famous athlete says…*), **loaded words** (*shocking, miracle*), **exaggeration** (*Chocolate cure!*) and the **curiosity gap** (*The surprising food that…*). For each one, ask: *What does the writer want me to feel, think or do? Is there real evidence?*","examples":["Point of view: Reem – ***advertising aimed at young children should be strictly limited***.","Purpose: headline article – ***to inform readers how headlines work and advise them to read carefully***.","Logic: ***Most children under about eight years old do not understand that advertisements are designed to persuade them.***","Emotion: ***A cartoon lion promised that it would make him 'super strong.'***","Trust/authority: ***The World Health Organization has warned…***","Rhetorical question (headline): ***Is Your Phone Making You Tired?***","Curiosity gap: ***The Surprising Food That Helps You Focus.***","Exaggeration / clickbait: ***Scientists Discover Chocolate Cure!***","Bandwagon (ad): ***Join millions of kids who already love it!***"],"common_errors":["✗ Mixing up the topic and the point of view. → ✓ Topic: advertising. Point of view: it should be limited. (Topic ≠ point of view.)","✗ Thinking every text with facts has the purpose *to inform*. → ✓ Writers also use facts to persuade. (Check the claim.)","✗ Calling every question a rhetorical question. → ✓ A rhetorical question does not expect an answer. (Know the difference.)","✗ Naming a technique without its effect. → ✓ Say what it makes the reader feel, think or do. (Technique + effect.)","✗ Believing a headline because it uses numbers. → ✓ Numbers can still be misleading – read the article. (Read beyond the headline.)","✗ The headline mislead readers last week. → ✓ The headline misled readers last week. (Irregular past: mislead → **misled**.)","✗ Emotional appeals are always bad. → ✓ They are fine if real evidence supports them. (Balance matters.)"],"arabic":"لكل كاتب وجهة نظر هي ما يؤمن به في الموضوع، وغرض: الإعلام أو الإقناع أو الإمتاع. وتكتشف وجهة النظر من الادعاء والأدلة التي يختارها الكاتب والكلمات التي يستخدمها.\n\nيستخدم الكتّاب ثلاث استمالات رئيسة: استمالة العقل بالحقائق والإحصاءات، واستمالة العاطفة بالقصص والمشاعر، واستمالة الثقة بالخبراء والمصادر الموثوقة.\n\nومن الأساليب الأخرى: الأسئلة البلاغية، واتباع الجماعة، والشهادات، والكلمات المشحونة، والمبالغة، وفجوة الفضول. واسأل عن كل أسلوب: ماذا يريدني الكاتب أن أشعر أو أفكر أو أفعل؟ وهل هناك دليل حقيقي؟\n\nانتبه: الموضوع غير وجهة النظر، والماضي من mislead هو misled."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:30","narration":"Shocking! You won't believe number 5! Why do some headlines make us click?","on_screen":"How Headlines Hook Readers 🎣","visual":"A fishing hook made of headline words pulls a cursor."},{"time":"00:30–01:40","narration":"Strong verbs, numbers, questions, curiosity gaps, emotional words – and sometimes clickbait.","on_screen":"Headline techniques","visual":"Headlines appear one by one with labels: VERB, NUMBER, QUESTION, CURIOSITY, EMOTION."},{"time":"01:40–02:40","narration":"Logic, emotion and trust – the three appeals every persuader uses.","on_screen":"Logic · Emotion · Trust","visual":"Three keys labeled LOGIC, EMOTION and TRUST open three doors."},{"time":"02:40–03:30","narration":"Reem wants to persuade. The headline article wants to inform and warn. Same topic – persuasion – different purposes!","on_screen":"Point of view and purpose","visual":"Two texts side by side with arrows to PERSUADE and INFORM."},{"time":"03:30–04:00","narration":"A headline is the door to a story, not the whole house. Read beyond it!","on_screen":"Read beyond the headline","visual":"A door opens to reveal a full article inside a house."}],"captions_ar":["صادم! لن تصدّق رقم 5! لماذا تجعلنا بعض العناوين ننقر؟","أفعال قوية، وأرقام، وأسئلة، وفجوات فضول، وكلمات عاطفية، وأحيانًا طُعم نقرات.","العقل والعاطفة والثقة: الاستمالات الثلاث التي يستخدمها كل مُقنِع.","ريم تريد الإقناع، ومقال العناوين يريد الإعلام والتحذير: الموضوع واحد والأغراض مختلفة!","العنوان باب القصة لا البيت كله. اقرأ ما بعده!"],"voice":"Narrator: American English, energetic, curious male voice, speed 1.0."},"texts":[{"type":"reading","title":"How Headlines Hook Readers","body":"Every day, thousands of headlines compete for our attention. A news website may publish hundreds of stories, but each reader clicks on only a few. That is why headline writers choose every word carefully. A good headline is short and clear, and, most importantly, it makes you want to read more.\n\n**Strong Verbs and Numbers** – Headlines often use powerful verbs. A storm does not just *arrive*; it *slams* the coast. Prices do not *rise*; they *soar*. Numbers make a story feel concrete and quick to read: *5 Ways to Sleep Better* promises useful information in a few minutes.\n\n**Questions and Curiosity** – Some headlines ask a question, such as *Is Your Phone Making You Tired?* Others leave out a key detail to create a \"curiosity gap\": *The Surprising Food That Helps You Focus.* Readers click because they want to find the missing answer.\n\n**Emotional Words** – Words such as *shocking*, *heartbreaking* and *miracle* appeal to our feelings. They can make a small story seem enormous and an ordinary event seem urgent. Capital letters and exclamation marks, as in *AMAZING NEWS!!!*, work in a similar way.\n\n**When Headlines Mislead** – Sometimes a headline promises much more than the article delivers. This is often called *clickbait*. For example, a headline might shout *Scientists Discover Chocolate Cure!* when the study only found a small effect in a few laboratory mice. Research suggests that many people share links without reading the article, so a misleading headline can spread false ideas very quickly.\n\n**Read Beyond the Headline** – Before you believe or share a story, read the whole article, check who wrote it and look for the evidence. If a headline makes you feel very angry or very excited, slow down, because strong feelings are a signal to check the facts. Ask yourself: *Does the article really support the headline? Who benefits if I click?* Remember that a headline is the door to a story, not the whole house.","word_count":320,"audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS3-T1","id":"TXT-RW-L07-U3-LS3-1","genre":"informational","role":"paired","difficulty":"800L–950L","glossary":[{"word":"concrete","definition":"clear and specific, not general","arabic":"ملموس / محدد"},{"word":"curiosity gap","definition":"missing information that makes you want to find out more","arabic":"فجوة الفضول"},{"word":"laboratory","definition":"a room where scientists do experiments","arabic":"مختبر"}]}],"activities":[{"id":"RW-L07-U3-LS3-A1","type":"mcq","stage":"controlled","instructions":"Controlled practice: Read *How Headlines Hook Readers*. Choose the answer.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ النص واختر الإجابة.","items":[{"q":"According to the article, what makes a good headline?","answer":"It is short and clear, and it makes you want to read more.","explanation":"Paragraph 1.","explanation_ar":"قصير وواضح ويشوّق.","options":["It is very long and detailed.","It is short and clear, and it makes you want to read more.","It never uses verbs.","It always includes a question."],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Which verb does the article give as a stronger way to say a storm *arrives*?","answer":"slams","explanation":"Strong Verbs and Numbers.","explanation_ar":"slams.","options":["soars","rises","focuses","slams"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What is a *curiosity gap*, according to the article?","answer":"a missing detail that makes readers want to click","explanation":"Questions and Curiosity.","explanation_ar":"فجوة الفضول.","options":["a mistake in a headline","a gap between two news stories","a missing detail that makes readers want to click","a long pause in a video"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary in context","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Which words does the article list as emotional words?","answer":"shocking, heartbreaking and miracle","explanation":"Emotional Words.","explanation_ar":"كلمات عاطفية.","options":["shocking, heartbreaking and miracle","five, ten and twenty","who, what and where","slams, soars and rises"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What did the study in the chocolate example actually find?","answer":"only a small effect in a few laboratory mice","explanation":"When Headlines Mislead.","explanation_ar":"أثر صغير في فئران قليلة.","options":["a cure for every disease","that chocolate is dangerous","only a small effect in a few laboratory mice","nothing about chocolate"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What does the article compare a headline to at the end?","answer":"the door to a story, not the whole house","explanation":"Read Beyond the Headline.","explanation_ar":"باب القصة.","options":["the door to a story, not the whole house","a key to a car","a window in a classroom","a map of a city"],"dok":1,"skill":"figurative language","stage":"controlled"}]},{"id":"RW-L07-U3-LS3-A2","type":"mcq","stage":"guided","instructions":"Guided practice: Point of view and techniques. Choose.","instructions_ar":"وجهة النظر والأساليب: اختر.","items":[{"q":"What is the MAIN purpose of *How Headlines Hook Readers*?","answer":"to inform readers how headlines work and warn them to read carefully","explanation":"Purpose.","explanation_ar":"الإعلام والتحذير.","options":["to sell a new phone","to inform readers how headlines work and warn them to read carefully","to entertain readers with a funny story","to persuade readers to stop reading news"],"dok":2,"skill":"author's purpose","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which technique does *Is Your Phone Making You Tired?* use?","answer":"a question to create curiosity","explanation":"Technique.","explanation_ar":"سؤال يثير الفضول.","options":["a testimonial","a bandwagon appeal","a statistic","a question to create curiosity"],"dok":2,"skill":"persuasive techniques","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which ad line uses the BANDWAGON technique?","answer":"Join the millions of families who already shop with us!","explanation":"Bandwagon.","explanation_ar":"اتباع الجماعة.","options":["Our juice contains 100 percent fruit.","A famous footballer says he drinks it every day.","Join the millions of families who already shop with us!","Don't you want the best for your children?"],"dok":2,"skill":"persuasive techniques","stage":"guided"},{"q":"In Reem's article, which sentence is an APPEAL TO TRUST (authority)?","answer":"The World Health Organization has warned that this kind of marketing influences what children want to eat.","explanation":"Appeal to trust.","explanation_ar":"استمالة الثقة.","options":["My brother is not unusual.","The World Health Organization has warned that this kind of marketing influences what children want to eat.","Parents cannot watch every screen at every moment.","Children deserve to grow up making choices based on facts."],"dok":2,"skill":"persuasive techniques","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which sentence about the misleading headline uses the correct past form?","answer":"The headline misled thousands of readers.","explanation":"Irregular verb.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["The headline misleaded thousands of readers.","The headline mislead thousands of readers yesterday.","The headline has misleaded thousands of readers.","The headline misled thousands of readers."],"dok":2,"skill":"grammar","stage":"guided"},{"q":"How are the two texts in this unit ALIKE?","answer":"Both warn readers that persuasive messages can influence them without their noticing.","explanation":"Comparing texts.","explanation_ar":"كلاهما يحذّر من تأثير رسائل الإقناع.","options":["Both warn readers that persuasive messages can influence them without their noticing.","Both are written to sell products.","Both are stories with characters.","Both argue that headlines should be banned."],"dok":3,"skill":"comparing texts","stage":"guided"}]},{"id":"RW-L07-U3-LS3-A3","type":"organizer","stage":"freer","instructions":"Organizer: Complete the Persuasion Detector.","instructions_ar":"أكمل كاشف الإقناع.","items":[{"q":"Reem's point of view and purpose.","answer":"She believes advertising aimed at young children should be strictly limited; her purpose is to persuade readers to support limits.","explanation":"Point of view.","explanation_ar":"وجهة النظر.","dok":2,"skill":"point of view","stage":"freer"},{"q":"One appeal to logic and one appeal to emotion from Reem's article, with their effects.","answer":"Logic: the 2004 task force finding about children under eight – it makes the argument reasonable. Emotion: her brother and the cartoon lion – it makes readers feel protective.","explanation":"Appeals.","explanation_ar":"الاستمالات.","dok":2,"skill":"persuasive techniques","stage":"freer"},{"q":"One appeal to trust and one loaded word from Reem's article, with their effects.","answer":"Trust: the World Health Organization – it makes the claim credible. Loaded word: 'unfair' – it makes readers feel that children are being treated wrongly.","explanation":"Trust + loaded words.","explanation_ar":"الثقة والكلمات المشحونة.","dok":3,"skill":"persuasive techniques","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Write the point of view and purpose of *How Headlines Hook Readers*.","answer":"The author thinks readers should be careful with headlines because they can mislead; the purpose is to inform and to advise readers to read beyond the headline.","explanation":"Point of view.","explanation_ar":"وجهة النظر.","dok":2,"skill":"point of view","stage":"freer"}]},{"id":"RW-L07-U3-LS3-A4","type":"open","stage":"production","instructions":"Writing: Analyze and compare.","instructions_ar":"حلّل وقارن.","items":[{"q":"Write two headlines about a school sports day: one accurate and one clickbait. Name the techniques in the clickbait headline.","answer":"Accurate: 'Grade 7 Wins School Sports Day Relay.' Clickbait: 'You Won't BELIEVE What Happened at Sports Day!' – curiosity gap and loaded capital letters.","explanation":"Headlines.","explanation_ar":"العناوين.","dok":2,"skill":"persuasive techniques","stage":"production"},{"q":"Explain why clickbait can be harmful.","answer":"It can spread false ideas, because many people share links without reading the article, and it wastes readers' time and trust.","explanation":"Effect.","explanation_ar":"الأثر.","dok":3,"skill":"evaluating media","stage":"production"},{"q":"Compare the purposes of the two texts in this unit.","answer":"Reem's article persuades readers to support limits on ads for children, while the headline article informs readers about headline techniques and advises them to read carefully.","explanation":"Compare.","explanation_ar":"قارن.","dok":3,"skill":"comparing texts","stage":"production"},{"q":"Which technique do you think works best on you? Why?","answer":"Curiosity gaps work on me, because when a headline hides an answer, I want to know it, even if the article is not useful.","explanation":"Reflect.","explanation_ar":"تأمّل.","dok":2,"skill":"connect","stage":"production"}]},{"id":"RW-L07-U3-LS3-TR-1","type":"timed_reading","instructions":"Timed reading: Read \"How Headlines Hook Readers\" aloud or silently. Try to finish in the time.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ النص وحاول أن تنهيه في الوقت المحدد.","items":[{"q":"Read “How Headlines Hook Readers”.","answer":"320 words","title":"How Headlines Hook Readers","body":"Every day, thousands of headlines compete for our attention. A news website may publish hundreds of stories, but each reader clicks on only a few. That is why headline writers choose every word carefully. A good headline is short and clear, and, most importantly, it makes you want to read more.\n\n**Strong Verbs and Numbers** – Headlines often use powerful verbs. A storm does not just *arrive*; it *slams* the coast. Prices do not *rise*; they *soar*. Numbers make a story feel concrete and quick to read: *5 Ways to Sleep Better* promises useful information in a few minutes.\n\n**Questions and Curiosity** – Some headlines ask a question, such as *Is Your Phone Making You Tired?* Others leave out a key detail to create a \"curiosity gap\": *The Surprising Food That Helps You Focus.* Readers click because they want to find the missing answer.\n\n**Emotional Words** – Words such as *shocking*, *heartbreaking* and *miracle* appeal to our feelings. They can make a small story seem enormous and an ordinary event seem urgent. Capital letters and exclamation marks, as in *AMAZING NEWS!!!*, work in a similar way.\n\n**When Headlines Mislead** – Sometimes a headline promises much more than the article delivers. This is often called *clickbait*. For example, a headline might shout *Scientists Discover Chocolate Cure!* when the study only found a small effect in a few laboratory mice. Research suggests that many people share links without reading the article, so a misleading headline can spread false ideas very quickly.\n\n**Read Beyond the Headline** – Before you believe or share a story, read the whole article, check who wrote it and look for the evidence. If a headline makes you feel very angry or very excited, slow down, because strong feelings are a signal to check the facts. Ask yourself: *Does the article really support the headline? Who benefits if I click?* Remember that a headline is the door to a story, not the whole house.","words":320,"limit":162}],"time_limit_sec":162},{"id":"RW-L07-U3-LS3-ORG","type":"organizer","organizer":"Persuasion Detector","instructions":"Organizer: Use Reem's article. Find the point of view, the purpose and an example of each technique, and explain its effect.","items":[{"q":"Author's point of view","answer":"Advertising aimed at young children should be strictly limited."},{"q":"Purpose","answer":"to persuade parents, schools and leaders to support limits"},{"q":"Appeal to logic (example + effect)","answer":"'most children under about eight years old do not understand that advertisements are designed to persuade them' – makes the argument sound reasonable"},{"q":"Appeal to emotion (example + effect)","answer":"the brother and the cartoon lion – makes readers feel protective"},{"q":"Appeal to trust (example + effect)","answer":"the American Psychological Association and the World Health Organization – makes the claim credible"},{"q":"Other techniques (loaded words, call to action)","answer":"loaded words: unfair, clever, protect; call to action: 'It is time for governments, companies and parents to work together'"}]}],"game":{"name":"Technique Detectives","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["On this screen: answer the lesson questions as fast as you can.","In class: Detective desk: a message appears on a phone screen; the child chooses the technique badge, then the effect (feel / think / do). Correct cases are stamped CASE CLOSED and added to a detective file.","A team draws an evidence card.","They choose the technique badge and explain its effect.","They decide whether the message is fair (evidence) or manipulative (no evidence).","Other teams may challenge with a better answer.","Correct technique = 1 point; correct effect = 1 point; fair/manipulative with a reason = 1 point."],"data":[]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"What is an author's *point of view*?","answer":"what the author believes about the topic","explanation":"Definition.","explanation_ar":"وجهة النظر.","options":["the place where the author lives","the number of paragraphs","what the author believes about the topic","the title of the text"],"dok":1,"skill":"point of view"},{"q":"What is *clickbait*?","answer":"a headline designed to make you click, often by exaggerating","explanation":"Vocabulary.","explanation_ar":"طُعم النقرات.","options":["a kind of computer mouse","a careful news report","a headline designed to make you click, often by exaggerating","a fishing website"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary"},{"q":"*The headline was **misleading**.* *Misleading* means ___.","answer":"giving a wrong idea","explanation":"Context.","explanation_ar":"مضلِّل.","options":["very accurate","short and clear","written by an expert","giving a wrong idea"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary in context"},{"q":"Which is an example of an appeal to EMOTION?","answer":"a sad story about a child who was tricked by an ad","explanation":"Appeal to emotion.","explanation_ar":"قصة مؤثرة.","options":["a statistic from a university study","a sad story about a child who was tricked by an ad","a quotation from a health organization","a list of dates"],"dok":1,"skill":"persuasive techniques"},{"q":"Which sentence uses the correct past form of *mislead*?","answer":"Last year, a fake headline misled many readers.","explanation":"Irregular verb.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["Last year, a fake headline misled many readers.","Last year, a fake headline misleaded many readers.","Last year, a fake headline misleading many readers.","Last year, a fake headline mislead many readers."],"dok":2,"skill":"grammar"},{"q":"Which technique does *A famous athlete says this drink gives him energy* use?","answer":"testimonial","explanation":"Testimonial.","explanation_ar":"شهادة.","options":["rhetorical question","testimonial","statistics","curiosity gap"],"dok":2,"skill":"persuasive techniques"},{"q":"*Don't our children deserve better?* What kind of question is this?","answer":"a rhetorical question that makes a point","explanation":"Rhetorical question.","explanation_ar":"سؤال بلاغي.","options":["a real question that needs a number as an answer","a test question","a clickbait headline","a rhetorical question that makes a point"],"dok":2,"skill":"persuasive techniques"},{"q":"Why does the headline article give the chocolate example?","answer":"to show how a headline can promise much more than the study found","explanation":"Author's purpose.","explanation_ar":"لإظهار مبالغة العنوان.","options":["to recommend eating chocolate","to explain how to do experiments","to show how a headline can promise much more than the study found","to describe laboratory mice"],"dok":2,"skill":"author's craft"},{"q":"Reem uses many appeals to emotion. Why does she ALSO include research and expert organizations?","answer":"so that her argument is supported by credible evidence, not only feelings","explanation":"Balance of appeals.","explanation_ar":"لتدعيم حجتها بأدلة موثوقة.","options":["so that her argument is supported by credible evidence, not only feelings","because emotions are not allowed in articles","to make the article shorter","because she has no point of view"],"dok":3,"skill":"evaluating arguments"},{"q":"What is the BEST thing to do before sharing a surprising headline?","answer":"Read the whole article, check the author and look for evidence.","explanation":"Media literacy.","explanation_ar":"اقرأ المقال كله وتحقّق.","options":["Read the whole article, check the author and look for evidence.","Share it quickly before others do.","Change the headline to make it more exciting.","Believe it if it has a number."],"dok":3,"skill":"evaluating media"}],"images":[{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS3-V01","prompt":"Picture for the word 'clickbait': a fishing hook holding a 'CLICK HERE' button"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS3-V02","prompt":"Picture for the word 'misleading': a road sign pointing the wrong way"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS3-V03","prompt":"Picture for the word 'technique': a toolbox with labeled tools"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS3-V04","prompt":"Picture for the word 'curiosity': a cat peeking into a box"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS3-V05","prompt":"Picture for the word 'bandwagon': a crowded wagon with people jumping on"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS3-V06","prompt":"Picture for the word 'testimonial': a famous player holding a product and smiling"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS3-V07","prompt":"Picture for the word 'rhetorical question': a speaker with a question mark that has no answer bubble"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS3-01","prompt":"Headlines designed to hook readers."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS3-02","prompt":"A misleading headline compared with the real finding."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS3-03","prompt":"The three appeals of persuasion."}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS3-V01","script":"clickbait. That headline is clickbait – the article says almost nothing."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS3-V02","script":"misleading. The headline was misleading."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS3-V03","script":"technique. A question is a common headline technique."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS3-V04","script":"curiosity. The headline made me click out of curiosity."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS3-V05","script":"bandwagon. 'Join millions of happy users!' is a bandwagon appeal."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS3-V06","script":"testimonial. The ad uses a testimonial from a famous athlete."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS3-V07","script":"rhetorical question. 'Don't our children deserve better?' is a rhetorical question."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS3-T1","script":"Every day, thousands of headlines compete for our attention. A news website may publish hundreds of stories, but each reader clicks on only a few. That is why headline writers choose every word carefully. A good headline is short and clear, and, most importantly, it makes you want to read more. **Strong Verbs and Numbers** – Headlines often use powerful verbs. A storm does not just *arrive*; it *slams* the coast. Prices do not *rise*; they *soar*. Numbers make a story feel concrete and quick to read: *5 Ways to Sleep Better* promises useful information in a few minutes. **Questions and Curiosity** – Some headlines ask a question, such as *Is Your Phone Making You Tired?* Others leave out a key detail to create a \"curiosity gap\": *The Surprising Food That Helps You Focus.* Readers click because they want to find the missing answer. **Emotional Words** – Words such as *shocking*, *heartbreaking* and *miracle* appeal to our feelings. They can make a small story seem enormous and an ordinary event seem urgent. Capital letters and exclamation marks, as in *AMAZING NEWS!!!*, work in a similar way. **When Headlines Mislead** – Sometimes a headline promises much more than the article delivers. This is often called *clickbait*. For example, a headline might shout *Scientists Discover Chocolate Cure!* when the study only found a small effect in a few laboratory mice. Research suggests that many people share links without reading the article, so a misleading headline can spread false ideas very quickly. **Read Beyond the Headline** – Before you believe or share a story, read the whole article, check who wrote it and look for the evidence. If a headline makes you feel very angry or very excited, slow down, because strong feelings are a signal to check the facts. Ask yourself: *Does the article really support the headline? Who benefits if I click?* Remember that a headline is the door to a story, not the whole house."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS3-VID","script":"Shocking! You won't believe number 5! Why do some headlines make us click? Strong verbs, numbers, questions, curiosity gaps, emotional words – and sometimes clickbait. Logic, emotion and trust – the three appeals every persuader uses. Reem wants to persuade. The headline article wants to inform and warn. Same topic – persuasion – different purposes! A headline is the door to a story, not the whole house. Read beyond it!"}]},{"id":"RW-L07-U3-LS4","title":"Writing Skill: Claim, reasons, evidence, counterclaim","lesson_type":"writing_skill","objectives":["write a clear, arguable claim and support it with two reasons","support each reason with evidence and explain how the evidence proves the reason","write a counterclaim with a concession and a rebuttal, and end with a call to action"],"objectives_ar":["كتابة ادعاء واضح قابل للنقاش ودعمه بسببين.","دعم كل سبب بدليل وشرح كيف يثبت الدليل السبب.","كتابة رأي مخالف مع إقرار وردّ، والختام بدعوة إلى العمل."],"success_criteria":["I can tell an arguable claim from a fact.","I can write a reason, a piece of evidence and an explanation sentence (*This shows that…*).","I can write *Some people argue… It is true that… However…* to answer the other side."],"success_criteria_ar":["أستطيع التمييز بين الادعاء القابل للنقاش والحقيقة.","أستطيع كتابة سبب ودليل وجملة تفسير.","أستطيع الرد على الرأي المخالف بهذه الصيغة."],"map_area":"Language Usage: Write, Revise Texts for Purpose and Audience (cross-strand)","vocabulary":[{"word":"claim","pos":"noun","ipa":"/kleɪm/","definition":"the main opinion that a writer argues for","arabic":"ادعاء / رأي محوري","example":"My claim is that every school needs a newspaper.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS4-V01","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS4-V01","tier":"core"},{"word":"counterclaim","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈkaʊntərˌkleɪm/","definition":"an opinion that disagrees with the writer's claim","arabic":"ادعاء مضاد / رأي مخالف","example":"A strong essay answers the counterclaim.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS4-V02","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS4-V02","tier":"core"},{"word":"rebuttal","pos":"noun","ipa":"/rɪˈbʌtəl/","definition":"an answer that shows why the other side's argument is weak or wrong","arabic":"دحض / رد","example":"Her rebuttal offered a simple solution.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS4-V03","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS4-V03","tier":"core"},{"word":"concede","pos":"verb","ipa":"/kənˈsid/","definition":"to admit that something is true, even if you do not like it","arabic":"يُقرّ / يسلّم بـ","example":"I concede that students are busy.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS4-V04","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS4-V04","tier":"core"},{"word":"transition","pos":"noun","ipa":"/trænˈzɪʃən/","definition":"a word or phrase that connects ideas, such as *first* or *however*","arabic":"أداة ربط / انتقال","example":"Use transitions to move between reasons.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS4-V05","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS4-V05","tier":"core"},{"word":"call to action","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌkɔl tu ˈækʃən/","definition":"a sentence that tells readers what they should do","arabic":"دعوة إلى العمل","example":"End your essay with a call to action.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS4-V06","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS4-V06","tier":"core"},{"word":"convincing","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/kənˈvɪnsɪŋ/","definition":"able to make people believe that something is true","arabic":"مُقنِع","example":"Specific evidence makes an argument convincing.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS4-V07","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS4-V07","tier":"core"}],"explanation":{"text":"An **argument** begins with a **claim** – an opinion that people **could disagree with** (*Every school should have a student newspaper*). A fact cannot be a claim (*Our school has a football team*). Make your claim **clear** and **specific**, and put it at the end of your introduction.\n\nSupport the claim with **two or three reasons**, introduced by **transitions** (*First, Second, In addition, Finally*). Give **evidence** for each reason: facts, statistics, examples or the words of an expert. Then **explain** the evidence – do not leave readers to guess: *This shows that… / This means that… / As a result, …*\n\nA **convincing** argument is **fair** to the other side. Write a **counterclaim** (*Some people argue that…*), **concede** what is true (*It is true that…*) and give a **rebuttal** (*However, …*). End with a **conclusion** that restates the claim in new words and a **call to action** (*Let's…, We should…, Join us…*).","examples":["Arguable claim: ***Every school should have a student newspaper.***","Not a claim (a fact): *Our school has a science club.*","Reason: ***First, a newspaper helps students become better writers.***","Evidence (example): ***Ms. Hanan noticed that her students' writing improved when they wrote for the class blog.***","Explanation: ***This shows that a real audience motivates students to do their best.***","Counterclaim: ***Some people argue that students are too busy to run a newspaper.***","Concession: ***It is true that students have a lot to do.***","Rebuttal: ***However, the newspaper could be published once a month.***","Call to action: ***Let's start one this year.***"],"common_errors":["✗ Writing a fact as a claim: *Many students use phones.* → ✓ Write an opinion people could debate: *Schools should teach phone safety.* (Arguable claim.)","✗ Giving reasons without evidence. → ✓ Add a fact, example or expert for each reason. (Reason + evidence.)","✗ Dropping evidence without explaining it. → ✓ Add *This shows that…* (Explain the evidence.)","✗ Ignoring the other side. → ✓ Add a counterclaim and a rebuttal. (Be fair and strong.)","✗ Attacking people: *People who disagree are silly.* → ✓ Answer the idea politely: *However, …* (Respect the other side.)","✗ Some people argues that students are busy. → ✓ Some people argue that students are busy. (Plural subject: **argue**.)","✗ Ending with *That's all.* → ✓ End with a restated claim and a call to action. (Strong conclusion.)"],"arabic":"تبدأ الحجة بادعاء: رأي يمكن أن يختلف معه الناس، أما الحقيقة فلا تصلح ادعاءً. اجعل ادعاءك واضحًا ومحددًا وضعه في نهاية المقدمة.\n\nادعم الادعاء بسببين أو ثلاثة تقدّمها بأدوات ربط (First, Second, In addition)، وقدّم دليلًا لكل سبب: حقائق أو إحصاءات أو أمثلة أو كلام خبير، ثم فسّر الدليل: This shows that…\n\nالحجة المقنعة عادلة مع الطرف الآخر: اذكر الرأي المخالف (Some people argue that…)، وأقرّ بما فيه من صواب (It is true that…)، ثم ردّ عليه (However…)، واختم بإعادة الادعاء بكلمات جديدة ودعوة إلى العمل.\n\nانتبه: ردّ على الفكرة لا على الأشخاص."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:30","narration":"You have an opinion. How do you make other people agree?","on_screen":"Claim · Reasons · Evidence · Counterclaim ⚖️","visual":"A student standing at a podium in a school hall."},{"time":"00:30–01:30","narration":"I believe that every school should have a student newspaper…","on_screen":"(model argument)","visual":"Students working on a newspaper; a teacher reading a blog post."},{"time":"01:30–02:40","narration":"First… For example… This shows that… Second… – reasons, evidence and explanations!","on_screen":"Reason → Evidence → Explanation","visual":"Three stacked blocks forming a strong tower."},{"time":"02:40–03:30","narration":"Some people argue… It is true that… However… – fair AND strong.","on_screen":"Counterclaim → Concession → Rebuttal","visual":"Two debaters shake hands, then one presents a solution card."},{"time":"03:30–04:00","narration":"Restate your claim – and tell readers what to do. Let's start!","on_screen":"Call to action","visual":"A newspaper landing on the principal's desk."}],"captions_ar":["لديك رأي. فكيف تجعل الآخرين يوافقونك؟","أرى أن كل مدرسة يجب أن تكون لها صحيفة طلابية…","أولًا… على سبيل المثال… وهذا يُظهر أن… ثانيًا…: أسباب وأدلة وتفسيرات!","يرى بعض الناس… صحيح أن… لكن…: عادل وقوي معًا.","أعد ادعاءك، وأخبر القارئ بما يفعل. هيا نبدأ!"],"voice":"Narrator: American English, confident teenage male voice for Turki; clear teacher voice for the explanation; speed 1.0."},"texts":[{"type":"model","title":"Every School Should Have a Student Newspaper (by Turki)","body":"Our school has a football team, a science club and a Quran circle, but it has no place where students can share their own news and ideas. I believe that every school should have a student newspaper.\n\nFirst, a newspaper helps students become better writers. When students know that real readers will see their work, they plan, check and revise more carefully. For example, our English teacher, Ms. Hanan, noticed that her students' writing improved when they wrote articles for the class blog last year. This shows that a real audience motivates students to do their best. Second, a newspaper teaches media literacy. Student reporters learn to check sources, separate facts from opinions and write accurate headlines. These skills matter in a world full of rumors and clickbait.\n\nSome people argue that students are too busy with schoolwork to run a newspaper. It is true that students have a lot to do. However, the newspaper could be published once a month, and each student would write only one or two articles a term.\n\nA student newspaper would give every student a voice and teach skills for life. Let's start one this year, so that the first issue can be on the principal's desk next month.","word_count":205,"audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS4-T1","id":"TXT-RW-L07-U3-LS4-1","genre":"opinion/argument","role":"model","difficulty":"800L–950L","glossary":[],"annotations":["**Hook + claim:** a clear, **arguable** opinion – *I believe that every school should have a student newspaper.*","**Reason 1 + evidence:** *First, a newspaper helps students become better writers.* → an example from a teacher.","**Explain the evidence:** *This shows that a real audience motivates students to do their best.*","**Reason 2 + evidence:** *Second, a newspaper teaches media literacy.* → specific skills.","**Counterclaim:** *Some people argue that students are too busy…*","**Concession + rebuttal:** *It is true that… However, …* – fair to the other side, then answered with a solution.","**Conclusion + call to action:** restate the claim and tell readers what to do – *Let's start one this year.*"]}],"activities":[{"id":"RW-L07-U3-LS4-A1","type":"mcq","stage":"controlled","instructions":"Controlled practice: Read Turki's argument. Choose the answer.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ حجة تركي واختر الإجابة.","items":[{"q":"What does Turki's school NOT have yet?","answer":"a student newspaper","explanation":"Paragraph 1.","explanation_ar":"صحيفة طلابية.","options":["a football team","a science club","a Quran circle","a student newspaper"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Who noticed that students' writing improved when they wrote for the class blog?","answer":"Ms. Hanan, the English teacher","explanation":"Paragraph 2.","explanation_ar":"المعلمة حنان.","options":["the principal","Ms. Hanan, the English teacher","Turki's father","a newspaper editor"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What skills does Turki say student reporters learn?","answer":"checking sources, separating facts from opinions and writing accurate headlines","explanation":"Paragraph 2.","explanation_ar":"مهارات الثقافة الإعلامية.","options":["playing football and swimming","cooking and cleaning","checking sources, separating facts from opinions and writing accurate headlines","drawing cartoons"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What counterclaim does Turki answer?","answer":"Students are too busy with schoolwork to run a newspaper.","explanation":"Paragraph 3.","explanation_ar":"الطلاب مشغولون.","options":["Newspapers are too expensive.","Students are too busy with schoolwork to run a newspaper.","Teachers do not like newspapers.","Students cannot write."],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"How often does Turki suggest publishing the newspaper?","answer":"once a month","explanation":"Paragraph 3.","explanation_ar":"مرة في الشهر.","options":["once a month","every day","once a year","every week"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Which sentence is Turki's call to action?","answer":"Let's start one this year, so that the first issue can be on the principal's desk next month.","explanation":"Call to action.","explanation_ar":"الدعوة إلى العمل.","options":["It is true that students have a lot to do.","First, a newspaper helps students become better writers.","Our school has a football team.","Let's start one this year, so that the first issue can be on the principal's desk next month."],"dok":2,"skill":"argument structure","stage":"controlled"}]},{"id":"RW-L07-U3-LS4-A2","type":"mcq","stage":"guided","instructions":"Guided practice: Build an argument. Choose.","instructions_ar":"ابنِ حجة: اختر.","items":[{"q":"Which sentence is an ARGUABLE claim about school lunches?","answer":"Schools should offer a vegetarian option every day.","explanation":"Arguable claim.","explanation_ar":"ادعاء قابل للنقاش.","options":["Schools should offer a vegetarian option every day.","The canteen opens at 9:30.","Our canteen sells sandwiches.","Lunch break is twenty minutes long."],"dok":2,"skill":"claims","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which is the BEST evidence for the reason *Reading for fun improves vocabulary*?","answer":"A study of thousands of students found that those who read for fun every day knew more words.","explanation":"Evidence.","explanation_ar":"أفضل دليل.","options":["I like reading comics.","Books are often colorful.","A study of thousands of students found that those who read for fun every day knew more words.","Some libraries are open on weekends."],"dok":2,"skill":"evidence","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which sentence EXPLAINS the evidence about Ms. Hanan's class?","answer":"This shows that a real audience motivates students to do their best.","explanation":"Explanation.","explanation_ar":"جملة التفسير.","options":["Ms. Hanan teaches English.","This shows that a real audience motivates students to do their best.","The class blog was online.","Last year was a busy year."],"dok":2,"skill":"evidence","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which rebuttal answers *A newspaper would cost too much money* BEST?","answer":"It is true that printing costs money. However, the newspaper could be published online for free.","explanation":"Rebuttal.","explanation_ar":"أفضل ردّ.","options":["It is true that printing costs money. However, the newspaper could be published online for free.","That is a silly thing to say.","Money is never important.","Newspapers are old-fashioned."],"dok":2,"skill":"counterclaims","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which sentence about the debate is grammatically correct?","answer":"Some parents argue that phones distract students.","explanation":"Subject–verb agreement.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["Some parents argues that phones distract students.","Some parents arguing that phones distract students.","Some parents argue that phones distract students.","Some parents is arguing that phones distract students."],"dok":2,"skill":"grammar","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Why does Turki write *It is true that students have a lot to do* before his rebuttal?","answer":"to show that he is fair to the other side, which makes readers trust him more","explanation":"Concession.","explanation_ar":"ليُظهر عدله فيكسب ثقة القارئ.","options":["to change his claim","to agree that the newspaper is a bad idea","to add a new reason","to show that he is fair to the other side, which makes readers trust him more"],"dok":3,"skill":"counterclaims","stage":"guided"}]},{"id":"RW-L07-U3-LS4-A3","type":"gap_fill","stage":"freer","instructions":"Freer practice: Complete the argument with the word bank.","instructions_ar":"أكمل الحجة من بنك الكلمات.","items":[{"q":"________, a school garden teaches students where food comes from.","answer":"First","explanation":"Transition.","explanation_ar":"أولًا.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"________ example, students in Grade 5 grew tomatoes and mint last spring.","answer":"For","explanation":"Evidence.","explanation_ar":"على سبيل.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"This ________ that students learn best by doing.","answer":"shows","explanation":"Explanation.","explanation_ar":"يُظهر.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Some people ________ that a garden takes too much time.","answer":"argue","explanation":"Counterclaim.","explanation_ar":"يرى.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"It is ________ that gardens need care every week.","answer":"true","explanation":"Concession.","explanation_ar":"صحيح.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"________, a team of volunteers could share the work.","answer":"However","explanation":"Rebuttal.","explanation_ar":"لكن.","stage":"freer"}]},{"id":"RW-L07-U3-LS4-A4","type":"open","stage":"production","instructions":"Writing: Write the parts of an argument.","instructions_ar":"اكتب أجزاء الحجة.","items":[{"q":"Write an arguable claim about screen time for students.","answer":"Students under thirteen should have no more than two hours of recreational screen time on school days.","explanation":"Claim.","explanation_ar":"الادعاء.","dok":2,"skill":"claims","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write one reason, one piece of evidence and one explanation sentence for your claim.","answer":"Reason: Too much screen time can hurt sleep. Evidence: Doctors advise turning off screens before bed because screen light can make it harder to fall asleep. Explanation: This shows that limiting screens can help students rest and learn better.","explanation":"Reason + evidence + explanation.","explanation_ar":"سبب ودليل وتفسير.","dok":3,"skill":"evidence","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write a counterclaim, a concession and a rebuttal for your claim.","answer":"Some people argue that screens are needed for homework. It is true that many assignments are online. However, the limit is only for recreational screen time, not schoolwork.","explanation":"Counterclaim + rebuttal.","explanation_ar":"الرأي المخالف والرد.","dok":3,"skill":"counterclaims","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write a conclusion with a call to action.","answer":"Limiting recreational screen time would help students sleep, focus and spend more time with their families. Let's agree on a family screen plan this week.","explanation":"Conclusion.","explanation_ar":"الخاتمة.","dok":2,"skill":"conclusions","stage":"production"}]}],"game":{"name":"Debate Chain","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["On this screen: answer the lesson questions as fast as you can.","In class: Chain builder: the child chooses the correct sentence for each link in the chain; a weak link (a fact instead of a claim, an opinion instead of evidence) breaks the chain and must be replaced.","Each team draws a topic card.","Team members take turns writing one link of the chain on the right color card.","The team reads the full chain aloud.","Other teams test the chain: is the claim arguable? Is the evidence real? Is the rebuttal polite?","Each strong link = 1 point; an unbroken chain = 5 bonus points."],"data":[]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"Which word means *the main opinion that a writer argues for*?","answer":"claim","explanation":"Claim.","explanation_ar":"الادعاء.","options":["claim","transition","headline","rumor"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary"},{"q":"What is a *rebuttal*?","answer":"an answer that shows why the other side's argument is weak","explanation":"Definition.","explanation_ar":"الرد.","options":["the first sentence of an essay","a piece of evidence","an answer that shows why the other side's argument is weak","a kind of headline"],"dok":1,"skill":"counterclaims"},{"q":"*I **concede** that the newspaper will take time.* *Concede* means ___.","answer":"admit that it is true","explanation":"Context.","explanation_ar":"أُقرّ.","options":["deny completely","forget","promise","admit that it is true"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary in context"},{"q":"Which word is a TRANSITION used to add a second reason?","answer":"Second,","explanation":"Transition.","explanation_ar":"أداة ربط.","options":["Second,","Claim","Rebuttal","Evidence"],"dok":1,"skill":"transitions"},{"q":"Which concession and rebuttal is punctuated correctly?","answer":"It is true that gardens need care. However, volunteers could help.","explanation":"Punctuation.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["It is true that gardens need care however volunteers could help.","It is true, that gardens need care. However volunteers could help.","It is true that gardens need care, However, volunteers could help.","It is true that gardens need care. However, volunteers could help."],"dok":2,"skill":"punctuation"},{"q":"Which sentence is a FACT, not an arguable claim?","answer":"The school library has 3,000 books.","explanation":"Fact vs. claim.","explanation_ar":"حقيقة.","options":["The library should open on weekends.","The school library has 3,000 books.","Every student should read for twenty minutes a day.","Schools need bigger libraries."],"dok":2,"skill":"claims"},{"q":"Why should you explain your evidence with *This shows that…*?","answer":"to make clear how the evidence proves your reason","explanation":"Explanation.","explanation_ar":"لتوضيح صلة الدليل بالسبب.","options":["to make the essay longer","to repeat the claim","to make clear how the evidence proves your reason","to introduce the counterclaim"],"dok":2,"skill":"evidence"},{"q":"Which is the BEST call to action for an essay about a school recycling program?","answer":"Let's place recycling bins in every classroom starting next week.","explanation":"Call to action.","explanation_ar":"أفضل دعوة إلى العمل.","options":["Let's place recycling bins in every classroom starting next week.","Recycling is a word.","Some people do not recycle.","The end."],"dok":2,"skill":"conclusions"},{"q":"A student writes *People who disagree with me are wrong and silly.* What is the BEST advice?","answer":"Answer the other side's idea politely with a concession and a rebuttal.","explanation":"Revising.","explanation_ar":"ردّ على الفكرة بأدب.","options":["Add more insults.","Remove the claim.","Answer the other side's idea politely with a concession and a rebuttal.","Write it in capital letters."],"dok":3,"skill":"counterclaims"},{"q":"Why does including a counterclaim make an argument MORE convincing?","answer":"It shows the writer understands the other side and can still defend the claim.","explanation":"Purpose.","explanation_ar":"يُظهر فهم الكاتب للطرف الآخر.","options":["It proves the writer is unsure.","It shows the writer understands the other side and can still defend the claim.","It makes the claim weaker.","It replaces the evidence."],"dok":3,"skill":"counterclaims"}],"images":[{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS4-V01","prompt":"Picture for the word 'claim': a flag planted on top of a hill"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS4-V02","prompt":"Picture for the word 'counterclaim': two flags on opposite hills"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS4-V03","prompt":"Picture for the word 'rebuttal': a tennis player returning a ball"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS4-V04","prompt":"Picture for the word 'concede': a person nodding while holding up one hand"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS4-V05","prompt":"Picture for the word 'transition': a bridge between two islands of text"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS4-V06","prompt":"Picture for the word 'call to action': a hand pointing forward with 'Let's start!'"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS4-V07","prompt":"Picture for the word 'convincing': a judge giving a thumbs-up"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS4-01","prompt":"Students creating a school newspaper."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS4-02","prompt":"The links of an argument."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS4-03","prompt":"A respectful debate with a concession and a rebuttal."}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS4-V01","script":"claim. My claim is that every school needs a newspaper."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS4-V02","script":"counterclaim. A strong essay answers the counterclaim."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS4-V03","script":"rebuttal. Her rebuttal offered a simple solution."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS4-V04","script":"concede. I concede that students are busy."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS4-V05","script":"transition. Use transitions to move between reasons."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS4-V06","script":"call to action. End your essay with a call to action."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS4-V07","script":"convincing. Specific evidence makes an argument convincing."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS4-T1","script":"Our school has a football team, a science club and a Quran circle, but it has no place where students can share their own news and ideas. I believe that every school should have a student newspaper. First, a newspaper helps students become better writers. When students know that real readers will see their work, they plan, check and revise more carefully. For example, our English teacher, Ms. Hanan, noticed that her students' writing improved when they wrote articles for the class blog last year. This shows that a real audience motivates students to do their best. Second, a newspaper teaches media literacy. Student reporters learn to check sources, separate facts from opinions and write accurate headlines. These skills matter in a world full of rumors and clickbait. Some people argue that students are too busy with schoolwork to run a newspaper. It is true that students have a lot to do. However, the newspaper could be published once a month, and each student would write only one or two articles a term. A student newspaper would give every student a voice and teach skills for life. Let's start one this year, so that the first issue can be on the principal's desk next month."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS4-VID","script":"You have an opinion. How do you make other people agree? I believe that every school should have a student newspaper… First… For example… This shows that… Second… – reasons, evidence and explanations! Some people argue… It is true that… However… – fair AND strong. Restate your claim – and tell readers what to do. Let's start!"}]},{"id":"RW-L07-U3-LS5","title":"Writing Workshop: A four-paragraph argument essay","lesson_type":"writing_workshop","objectives":["plan an argument essay on a school issue with the Argument Essay Planner","write four paragraphs (200–250 words) with a claim, two reasons with evidence and explanations, a counterclaim with a rebuttal and a call to action","revise for fair, convincing language, edit and present the essay in a class debate"],"objectives_ar":["التخطيط لمقالة حجاجية عن قضية مدرسية بمخطط المقالة الحجاجية.","كتابة أربع فقرات (200–250 كلمة) تتضمن ادعاءً وسببين بأدلة وتفسيرات ورأيًا مخالفًا مع الرد ودعوة إلى العمل.","تحسين المقالة لتكون لغتها عادلة ومقنعة، وتدقيقها، وعرضها في مناظرة صفية."],"success_criteria":["My introduction has a hook and a clear, arguable claim.","My second paragraph gives two reasons, each with evidence and an explanation sentence.","My third paragraph answers a counterclaim fairly; my conclusion has a call to action; the essay is 200–250 words."],"success_criteria_ar":["مقدمتي فيها افتتاحية جاذبة وادعاء واضح قابل للنقاش.","فقرتي الثانية تقدّم سببين لكل منهما دليل وجملة تفسير.","فقرتي الثالثة تردّ على الرأي المخالف بعدل، وخاتمتي فيها دعوة إلى العمل، والمقالة في 200–250 كلمة."],"map_area":"Language Usage: Write, Revise Texts for Purpose and Audience (cross-strand)","vocabulary":[{"word":"debate","pos":"noun / verb","ipa":"/dɪˈbeɪt/","definition":"a formal discussion in which people give opposing opinions; to discuss in this way","arabic":"مناظرة / يتناظر","example":"Our class held a debate about phones.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS5-V01","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS5-V01","tier":"core"},{"word":"distraction","pos":"noun","ipa":"/dɪˈstrækʃən/","definition":"something that takes your attention away from what you are doing","arabic":"مُشتِّت / تشتيت","example":"Phones can be a distraction in class.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS5-V02","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS5-V02","tier":"core"},{"word":"concentration","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌkɑnsənˈtreɪʃən/","definition":"the ability to give all your attention to something","arabic":"تركيز","example":"Notifications break your concentration.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS5-V03","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS5-V03","tier":"core"},{"word":"survey","pos":"noun / verb","ipa":"/ˈsɝveɪ/","definition":"a set of questions you ask many people to find out their opinions; to ask these questions","arabic":"استطلاع / يستطلع","example":"We did a survey of 100 students.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS5-V04","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS5-V04","tier":"core"},{"word":"statistic","pos":"noun","ipa":"/stəˈtɪstɪk/","definition":"a number that shows a fact about a group","arabic":"إحصائية","example":"One statistic showed that most students slept less than eight hours.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS5-V05","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS5-V05","tier":"core"},{"word":"notification","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌnoʊtəfəˈkeɪʃən/","definition":"a message or a sound on a phone that tells you something new has happened","arabic":"إشعار / تنبيه","example":"A notification lit up her screen.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS5-V06","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS5-V06","tier":"core"},{"word":"trial","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈtraɪəl/","definition":"a short test period to see whether something works","arabic":"تجربة / فترة تجريبية","example":"Let's try a phone-free trial for one month.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS5-V07","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS5-V07","tier":"core"}],"explanation":{"text":"An **argument essay** tries to **persuade** readers to accept a **claim** about an issue. A strong essay is **logical** (reasons + evidence), **fair** (it answers the other side) and **clear** (it uses transitions and ends with a call to action).\n\n**Paragraph 1 – Introduction:** a **hook** (a scene, a question or a surprising fact) and your **claim**. **Paragraph 2 – Reasons:** *First, … For example, … This shows that… Second, …* **Paragraph 3 – Counterclaim:** *Some people argue that… It is true that… However, …* **Paragraph 4 – Conclusion:** restate the claim in new words and add a **call to action** (*Let's… / We should…*).\n\nUse **credible evidence** – from the topic card, a survey you carry out or a reliable source – and **cite** it (*In 2023, UNESCO recommended…*). Never invent statistics. Use persuasive language **fairly**: modal verbs (*should, must*), inclusive words (*we, our*) and a few loaded words, but **no insults**. Keep the essay **200–250 words**.","examples":["Hook (a scene): ***The bell rings for break, and half the students in the courtyard are staring at their screens.***","Claim: ***Schools should ban personal phones during school hours, except when teachers use them for learning.***","Reason 1: ***First, phones distract students from learning.***","Evidence (credible source): ***In 2023, UNESCO's Global Education Monitoring Report recommended that smartphones be used in schools only when they support learning.***","Explanation: ***This shows that experts are worried about the effect of phones on concentration.***","Reason 2: ***Second, a phone-free day encourages real friendships.***","Counterclaim + concession: ***Some people argue that students need phones in an emergency. It is true that families must be able to reach their children.***","Rebuttal: ***However, students could keep their phones in a locker, and parents can always call the school directly.***","Call to action: ***Let's ask our student council to propose a phone-free trial for one month.***"],"common_errors":["✗ No clear claim: *Phones are interesting.* → ✓ State a side: *Schools should ban personal phones during school hours.* (Arguable claim.)","✗ Making up numbers: *99% of students are addicted.* → ✓ Use real evidence from the card or a real survey. (Never invent statistics.)","✗ Evidence with no explanation. → ✓ Add *This shows that…* (Explain.)","✗ Skipping the other side. → ✓ Add a counterclaim, a concession and a rebuttal. (Fair and strong.)","✗ Phones is a big distraction. → ✓ Phones are a big distraction. (Plural subject: **are**.)","✗ Too many loaded words: *evil, horrible, disgusting phones*. → ✓ Use a few strong words and let the evidence persuade. (Persuade fairly.)","✗ Writing 350 words. → ✓ Keep it 200–250 words. (Check the length.)"],"arabic":"المقالة الحجاجية تحاول إقناع القارئ بادعاء حول قضية ما، والمقالة القوية منطقية (أسباب وأدلة)، وعادلة (ترد على الطرف الآخر)، وواضحة (أدوات ربط ودعوة إلى العمل).\n\nالفقرة الأولى: افتتاحية جاذبة والادعاء. الثانية: الأسباب مع الأدلة والتفسير. الثالثة: الرأي المخالف والإقرار والرد. الرابعة: إعادة الادعاء بكلمات جديدة ودعوة إلى العمل.\n\nاستخدم أدلة موثوقة من البطاقة أو استطلاع تجريه أو مصدر موثوق ووثّقها، ولا تخترع إحصاءات أبدًا. واستخدم لغة الإقناع بعدل دون إهانات، والتزم بطول 200–250 كلمة.\n\nانتبه: phones are (جمع) لا phones is."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:30","narration":"Phones in school? Later start times? Food ads near schools? Volunteering for everyone? Pick a side!","on_screen":"My Argument Essay 🎤","visual":"Four debate cards spin and land on a desk."},{"time":"00:30–01:20","narration":"Agree or disagree – both sides can win if the argument is strong.","on_screen":"Choose your side","visual":"A student moves to the AGREE side of the room."},{"time":"01:20–02:40","narration":"The bell rings for break, and half the students in the courtyard are staring at their screens…","on_screen":"(model essay)","visual":"Students in a courtyard with phones; then the same students playing and talking."},{"time":"02:40–03:30","narration":"Claim. Reasons with evidence. Counterclaim and rebuttal. Call to action.","on_screen":"P1 Claim · P2 Reasons · P3 Counterclaim · P4 Call to action","visual":"Four steps of a staircase light up."},{"time":"03:30–04:00","narration":"Bring your essay to our class debate – and may the best argument win!","on_screen":"Class debate 🏆","visual":"Two teams at podiums; the audience raises voting cards."}],"captions_ar":["الهواتف في المدرسة؟ تأخير بدء اليوم الدراسي؟ إعلانات الطعام قرب المدارس؟ التطوع للجميع؟ اختر موقفك!","مؤيد أو معارض: كلا الطرفين يمكن أن يفوز إذا كانت الحجة قوية.","يرن جرس الاستراحة، ونصف الطلاب في الساحة يحدّقون في شاشاتهم…","الادعاء، ثم الأسباب بالأدلة، ثم الرأي المخالف والرد، ثم الدعوة إلى العمل.","أحضر مقالتك إلى المناظرة الصفية، ولتفُز الحجة الأقوى!"],"voice":"Narrator: American English, motivating female voice; a student reads the model essay; speed 1.0."},"texts":[{"type":"model","title":"Debate Topic Cards","body":"**Card A – Should schools ban personal phones during the school day?** · Evidence to consider: In 2023, UNESCO's Global Education Monitoring Report recommended that smartphones be used in schools only when they support learning. · Phones can help in emergencies and with some school research. · Notifications can interrupt concentration.\n\n**Card B – Should middle and high schools start later in the morning?** · Evidence to consider: In 2014, the American Academy of Pediatrics recommended that middle and high schools start at 8:30 a.m. or later. · Sleep experts say teenagers need about 8–10 hours of sleep a night. · A later start could make the school day end later and affect family plans and traffic.\n\n**Card C – Should advertisements for unhealthy food be banned near schools?** · Evidence to consider: In 2023, the World Health Organization recommended strong policies to protect children from harmful food marketing. · Shops near schools depend on student customers. · Healthy foods could still be advertised.\n\n**Card D – Should every student do volunteer work during the school year?** · Evidence to consider: Saudi Vision 2030 set a goal of reaching one million volunteers a year. · Volunteering can build skills such as teamwork and leadership. · Some students already have heavy schedules.","word_count":198,"audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS5-T1","id":"TXT-RW-L07-U3-LS5-1","genre":"topic cards","role":"model","difficulty":"800L–950L","glossary":[],"annotations":["Choose **one card** and **decide your side** – agree or disagree. Both sides can be argued well.","Plan **four paragraphs**: (1) hook + **claim**; (2) **two reasons**, each with **evidence** and an **explanation**; (3) **counterclaim**, **concession** and **rebuttal**; (4) conclusion with a **call to action**.","Use the evidence on the card (and your own examples), **cite** where facts come from, and use persuasive language **fairly** – no insults, no made-up statistics."]}],"activities":[{"id":"RW-L07-U3-LS5-A1","type":"mcq","stage":"controlled","instructions":"Controlled practice: Read the topic cards and the model. Choose the answer.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ بطاقات الموضوعات والنموذج واختر الإجابة.","items":[{"q":"Which organization's 2023 report is mentioned on Card A?","answer":"UNESCO","explanation":"Card A.","explanation_ar":"اليونسكو.","options":["the World Health Organization","UNESCO","the American Academy of Pediatrics","Saudi Vision 2030"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What start time did the American Academy of Pediatrics recommend for middle and high schools, according to Card B?","answer":"8:30 a.m. or later","explanation":"Card B.","explanation_ar":"8:30 أو بعدها.","options":["6:30 a.m. or earlier","10:00 a.m. exactly","8:30 a.m. or later","noon"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"About how many hours of sleep do teenagers need, according to sleep experts on Card B?","answer":"about 8–10 hours","explanation":"Card B.","explanation_ar":"8–10 ساعات.","options":["about 4–5 hours","about 12–14 hours","about 6 hours","about 8–10 hours"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What volunteer goal did Saudi Vision 2030 set, according to Card D?","answer":"one million volunteers a year","explanation":"Card D.","explanation_ar":"مليون متطوع سنويًا.","options":["one thousand volunteers a year","ten volunteers in every school","no goal at all","one million volunteers a year"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"In the model essay, where could students keep their phones during the day?","answer":"in a locker or at the school office","explanation":"Paragraph 3.","explanation_ar":"في الخزانة أو مكتب المدرسة.","options":["in a locker or at the school office","in their pockets on silent","at the canteen","in the courtyard"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Which sentence in the model essay is the CLAIM?","answer":"Schools should ban personal phones during school hours, except when teachers use them for learning.","explanation":"Claim.","explanation_ar":"الادعاء.","options":["Phones are useful tools.","Schools should ban personal phones during school hours, except when teachers use them for learning.","Without screens, students talk, play and solve problems together.","It is true that families must be able to reach their children."],"dok":2,"skill":"argument structure","stage":"controlled"}]},{"id":"RW-L07-U3-LS5-A2","type":"mcq","stage":"guided","instructions":"Guided practice: Plan your argument essay. Choose.","instructions_ar":"خطّط لمقالتك الحجاجية: اختر.","items":[{"q":"Which is the BEST claim for the DISAGREE side of Card B?","answer":"Schools should keep their current start times because a later start would make the whole day end too late.","explanation":"Claim.","explanation_ar":"أفضل ادعاء.","options":["Sleep is important.","Schools should keep their current start times because a later start would make the whole day end too late.","Some schools start at 7:00.","I am not sure about school times."],"dok":2,"skill":"claims","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which sentence uses Card C's evidence correctly and cites it?","answer":"In 2023, the World Health Organization recommended strong policies to protect children from harmful food marketing.","explanation":"Evidence + citation.","explanation_ar":"دليل موثّق.","options":["Everyone knows food ads are evil.","In 2023, the World Health Organization recommended strong policies to protect children from harmful food marketing.","Shops sell food.","Many people like snacks."],"dok":2,"skill":"evidence","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which counterclaim would a writer AGREEING with Card D most likely answer?","answer":"Some students already have heavy schedules and no time to volunteer.","explanation":"Counterclaim.","explanation_ar":"الرأي المخالف المتوقع.","options":["Volunteering builds teamwork.","Vision 2030 has a volunteer goal.","Volunteering helps the community.","Some students already have heavy schedules and no time to volunteer."],"dok":2,"skill":"counterclaims","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which sentence about phones in class is grammatically correct?","answer":"Phones are a big distraction for many students.","explanation":"Agreement.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["Phones are a big distraction for many students.","Phones is a big distraction for many students.","Phones are a big distractions for many students.","Phones be a big distraction for many students."],"dok":2,"skill":"grammar","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which call to action BEST ends an essay that supports Card C?","answer":"Let's write to our city council and ask for a ban on junk-food ads within 500 meters of schools.","explanation":"Call to action.","explanation_ar":"أفضل دعوة إلى العمل.","options":["Food is important.","Some ads are colorful.","Let's write to our city council and ask for a ban on junk-food ads within 500 meters of schools.","That's my opinion."],"dok":2,"skill":"conclusions","stage":"guided"},{"q":"A partner writes *Ninety-nine percent of students are addicted to phones* with no source. What is the BEST advice?","answer":"Remove the invented statistic or replace it with real evidence and its source.","explanation":"Revising.","explanation_ar":"احذف الإحصائية المخترعة.","options":["Make the number bigger.","Keep it – it sounds convincing.","Remove the invented statistic or replace it with real evidence and its source.","Add more exclamation marks."],"dok":3,"skill":"evaluating evidence","stage":"guided"}]},{"id":"RW-L07-U3-LS5-A3","type":"organizer","stage":"freer","instructions":"Organizer: Plan with the Argument Essay Planner.","instructions_ar":"خطط بمخطط المقالة الحجاجية.","items":[{"q":"Card B (AGREE): write a hook and a claim.","answer":"Hook: At 6:15 a.m., most teenagers in our class are still half asleep at the breakfast table. Claim: Middle and high schools should start later in the morning.","explanation":"Hook + claim.","explanation_ar":"الافتتاحية والادعاء.","dok":2,"skill":"planning","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Card B (AGREE): write two reasons with evidence.","answer":"Reason 1: Teenagers need more sleep – sleep experts say about 8–10 hours a night. Reason 2: Doctors support later starts – in 2014, the American Academy of Pediatrics recommended 8:30 a.m. or later.","explanation":"Reasons + evidence.","explanation_ar":"الأسباب والأدلة.","dok":2,"skill":"planning","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Card B (AGREE): write a counterclaim, a concession and a rebuttal.","answer":"Some people argue that a later start would make the day end later. It is true that afternoons would be busier. However, schools could shorten breaks slightly, and rested students learn more efficiently.","explanation":"Counterclaim + rebuttal.","explanation_ar":"الرأي المخالف والرد.","dok":3,"skill":"planning","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Card B (AGREE): write a conclusion with a call to action.","answer":"Starting later would help students sleep, focus and stay healthy. Let's ask our school to try an 8:00 start for one term and measure the results.","explanation":"Conclusion.","explanation_ar":"الخاتمة.","dok":2,"skill":"planning","stage":"freer"}]},{"id":"RW-L07-U3-LS5-A4","type":"writing","stage":"production","instructions":"Writing: Write, revise, edit and debate.","instructions_ar":"اكتب وحسّن ودقق وناظر.","items":[{"q":"Write Paragraph 1 (hook and claim) for your chosen card.","answer":"At 6:15 a.m., most teenagers in our class are still half asleep at the breakfast table. They are not lazy; their bodies simply need more rest. Middle and high schools should start later in the morning.","explanation":"Paragraph 1.","explanation_ar":"الفقرة الأولى.","dok":2,"skill":"writing","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write Paragraph 2 (two reasons with evidence and explanations).","answer":"First, teenagers need more sleep. Sleep experts say that teens need about eight to ten hours a night, but early starts make this almost impossible. This shows that early schedules work against students' health. Second, doctors support later starts: in 2014, the American Academy of Pediatrics recommended that middle and high schools begin at 8:30 a.m. or later.","explanation":"Paragraph 2.","explanation_ar":"الفقرة الثانية.","dok":2,"skill":"writing","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write Paragraphs 3 and 4 (counterclaim, rebuttal and call to action).","answer":"Some people argue that a later start would make the school day end too late. It is true that afternoons would be busier. However, schools could shorten breaks, and rested students learn faster. A later start would help students stay healthy and focused. Let's ask our school to test an 8:00 start for one term.","explanation":"Paragraphs 3–4.","explanation_ar":"الفقرتان الثالثة والرابعة.","dok":3,"skill":"writing","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write one sentence you changed to make your language fairer or more convincing (before → after).","answer":"Before: Early school is torture for teenagers! After: Early start times make it difficult for many teenagers to get enough sleep.","explanation":"Revision.","explanation_ar":"التحسين.","dok":3,"skill":"revising","stage":"production","_w":{"checklist":[],"min":null,"max":null}}]},{"id":"RW-L07-U3-LS5-ORG","type":"organizer","organizer":"Argument Essay Planner","instructions":"Organizer: Choose one card and one side. Plan each part before you draft. Use real evidence only.","items":[{"q":"Hook + claim","answer":"Hook: students staring at screens at break. Claim: schools should ban personal phones during school hours, except for learning."},{"q":"Reason 1 + evidence + explanation","answer":"Phones distract students – UNESCO 2023 report: phones in school only when they support learning → experts worry about concentration"},{"q":"Reason 2 + evidence + explanation","answer":"Phone-free days build friendships – students talk and play at break (my own observation)"},{"q":"Counterclaim + concession","answer":"Students need phones in emergencies; it is true that families must reach their children."},{"q":"Rebuttal","answer":"Phones in lockers or at the office; parents can call the school directly."},{"q":"Conclusion + call to action","answer":"A phone-free day helps focus and friendship. Let's ask the student council for a one-month phone-free trial."}]},{"id":"RW-L07-U3-LS5-WR","type":"writing","instructions":"Writing: Choose one Debate Topic Card and decide your side. Write an argument essay (200–250 words) in four paragraphs: (1) a hook and a clear claim; (2) two reasons, each with evidence and an explanation; (3) a counterclaim, a concession and a rebuttal; (4) a conclusion that restates your claim and gives a call to action. Use only real evidence and keep your language fair. Choose one Debate Topic Card and decide your side. Write an argument essay (200–250 words) in four paragraphs: (1) a hook and a clear claim; (2) two reasons, each with evidence and an explanation; (3) a counterclaim, a concession and a rebuttal; (4) a conclusion that restates your claim and gives a call to action. Use only real evidence and keep your language fair.","instructions_ar":"اختر بطاقة من بطاقات موضوعات المناظرة وحدّد موقفك، ثم اكتب مقالة حجاجية (200–250 كلمة) في أربع فقرات: (1) افتتاحية جاذبة وادعاء واضح، (2) سببين لكل منهما دليل وتفسير، (3) رأيًا مخالفًا وإقرارًا وردًا، (4) خاتمة تعيد الادعاء وتتضمن دعوة إلى العمل. استخدم أدلة حقيقية فقط وحافظ على لغة عادلة.","prompt":"Choose one Debate Topic Card and decide your side. Write an argument essay (200–250 words) in four paragraphs: (1) a hook and a clear claim; (2) two reasons, each with evidence and an explanation; (3) a counterclaim, a concession and a rebuttal; (4) a conclusion that restates your claim and gives a call to action. Use only real evidence and keep your language fair.","checklist":["My introduction has a hook and a clear, arguable claim.","I gave two reasons, introduced with transitions.","Each reason has real evidence and an explanation sentence.","I included a counterclaim, a concession and a rebuttal.","My conclusion restates the claim in new words and has a call to action.","I used persuasive language fairly – no insults and no invented statistics.","I checked subject–verb agreement and punctuation after transitions.","My essay is 200–250 words, and I checked spelling."],"rubric_id":"RW-RUBRIC-5T","min_words":200,"max_words":275,"organizer":"Argument Essay Planner (hook and claim – reasons and evidence – counterclaim – rebuttal – call to action)","items":[{"q":"Choose one Debate Topic Card and decide your side. Write an argument essay (200–250 words) in four paragraphs: (1) a hook and a clear claim; (2) two reasons, each with evidence and an explanation; (3) a counterclaim, a concession and a rebuttal; (4) a conclusion that restates your claim and gives a call to action. Use only real evidence and keep your language fair.","answer":"The bell rings for break, and instead of talking or playing, half the students in the courtyard are staring at their screens. Phones are useful tools, but they do not belong in every part of the school day. Schools should ban personal phones during school hours, except when teachers use them for learning. (new paragraph) First, phones distract students from learning. A single notification can pull a student's attention away from a lesson, and it takes time to focus again. In 2023, UNESCO's Global Education Monitoring Report recommended that smartphones be used in schools only when they support learning. This shows that experts are worried about the effect of phones on concentration. Second, a phone-free day encourages real friendships. Without screens, students talk, play and solve problems together at break, which helps them build social skills. (new paragraph) Some people argue that students need phones to contact their parents in an emergency. It is true that families must be able to reach their children. However, students could keep their phones in a locker or hand them to the office in the morning, and parents can always call the school directly. (new paragraph) A phone-free school day would help students focus in class and connect with one another at break. Let's ask our student council to propose a phone-free trial for one month so that we can see the difference for ourselves.","_w":{"checklist":["My introduction has a hook and a clear, arguable claim.","I gave two reasons, introduced with transitions.","Each reason has real evidence and an explanation sentence.","I included a counterclaim, a concession and a rebuttal.","My conclusion restates the claim in new words and has a call to action.","I used persuasive language fairly – no insults and no invented statistics.","I checked subject–verb agreement and punctuation after transitions.","My essay is 200–250 words, and I checked spelling."],"min":200,"max":275}}]}],"game":{"name":"Four Corners Debate","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["On this screen: answer the lesson questions as fast as you can.","In class: Debate arena: the child chooses a side, then picks the strongest reason, the best evidence and the fairest rebuttal from options; the audience meter rises for logic and evidence and falls for insults or invented numbers.","Students go to the corner of the card they wrote about and split into AGREE and DISAGREE.","One speaker from each side reads a claim and one reason with evidence (one minute).","The rebuttal ball is passed to the other side, which must answer with 'It is true that… However…'.","The audience scores each side for logic, evidence and fairness.","Each score card point = 1 point; an insult or an invented statistic = minus 2 points."],"data":[]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"Which word means *a formal discussion with opposing opinions*?","answer":"debate","explanation":"Debate.","explanation_ar":"مناظرة.","options":["survey","trial","debate","notification"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary"},{"q":"What is a *statistic*?","answer":"a number that shows a fact about a group","explanation":"Vocabulary.","explanation_ar":"إحصائية.","options":["a kind of opinion","a number that shows a fact about a group","a short slogan","a call to action"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary"},{"q":"*Let's try a phone-free **trial** for one month.* *Trial* means ___.","answer":"a short test period to see whether something works","explanation":"Context.","explanation_ar":"فترة تجريبية.","options":["a court case","a short test period to see whether something works","a long holiday","a type of phone"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary in context"},{"q":"Where should the claim usually appear in an argument essay?","answer":"in the introduction","explanation":"Structure.","explanation_ar":"في المقدمة.","options":["in the introduction","only in the conclusion","in the counterclaim paragraph","nowhere"],"dok":1,"skill":"argument structure"},{"q":"Which sentence about notifications is correct?","answer":"Notifications break students' concentration.","explanation":"Agreement.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["Notifications breaks students' concentration.","Notifications breaking students' concentration.","Notifications break students' concentration.","Notifications is break students' concentration."],"dok":2,"skill":"grammar"},{"q":"Which is a CREDIBLE piece of evidence for an essay about school start times?","answer":"a recommendation from the American Academy of Pediatrics","explanation":"Credible evidence.","explanation_ar":"دليل موثوق.","options":["a comment from an anonymous website","my cousin's guess","a cartoon about sleepy students","a recommendation from the American Academy of Pediatrics"],"dok":2,"skill":"evaluating evidence"},{"q":"What should come right after a piece of evidence in a body paragraph?","answer":"an explanation of how it supports the reason","explanation":"Structure.","explanation_ar":"التفسير.","options":["an explanation of how it supports the reason","the call to action","a new claim","the title"],"dok":2,"skill":"evidence"},{"q":"Which sentence uses persuasive language FAIRLY?","answer":"A later start would help many students get the sleep they need.","explanation":"Fair language.","explanation_ar":"لغة عادلة.","options":["A later start would help many students get the sleep they need.","Only foolish people want early school.","Early school is the worst idea in history.","Everyone in the world agrees with me."],"dok":2,"skill":"persuasive language"},{"q":"A student's essay has a great claim and three reasons but no counterclaim. What is the BEST advice?","answer":"Add a counterclaim, a concession and a rebuttal to show fairness.","explanation":"Revising.","explanation_ar":"أضف الرأي المخالف والرد.","options":["Add a fourth reason instead.","Remove one reason.","Write the claim again in the conclusion only.","Add a counterclaim, a concession and a rebuttal to show fairness."],"dok":3,"skill":"counterclaims"},{"q":"How does this lesson connect to the unit question *How can words change minds and lives?*","answer":"Well-chosen words and real evidence can persuade people to change rules and habits.","explanation":"Theme connection.","explanation_ar":"الكلمات المختارة والأدلة تغيّر القرارات.","options":["Words cannot change anything.","Only loud words persuade people.","Arguments are only for lawyers.","Well-chosen words and real evidence can persuade people to change rules and habits."],"dok":3,"skill":"theme"}],"images":[{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS5-V01","prompt":"Picture for the word 'debate': two teams at podiums facing each other"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS5-V02","prompt":"Picture for the word 'distraction': a student looking at a phone instead of the board"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS5-V03","prompt":"Picture for the word 'concentration': a student focused on a book with a calm face"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS5-V04","prompt":"Picture for the word 'survey': a clipboard with a checklist and tally marks"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS5-V05","prompt":"Picture for the word 'statistic': a bar chart on a whiteboard"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS5-V06","prompt":"Picture for the word 'notification': a phone with a red notification badge"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS5-V07","prompt":"Picture for the word 'trial': a calendar with one month circled"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS5-01","prompt":"A class debate."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS5-02","prompt":"Four debate topic cards."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U3-LS5-03","prompt":"A staircase-shaped argument planner."}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS5-V01","script":"debate. Our class held a debate about phones."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS5-V02","script":"distraction. Phones can be a distraction in class."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS5-V03","script":"concentration. Notifications break your concentration."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS5-V04","script":"survey. We did a survey of 100 students."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS5-V05","script":"statistic. One statistic showed that most students slept less than eight hours."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS5-V06","script":"notification. A notification lit up her screen."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS5-V07","script":"trial. Let's try a phone-free trial for one month."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS5-T1","script":"**Card A – Should schools ban personal phones during the school day?** · Evidence to consider: In 2023, UNESCO's Global Education Monitoring Report recommended that smartphones be used in schools only when they support learning. · Phones can help in emergencies and with some school research. · Notifications can interrupt concentration. **Card B – Should middle and high schools start later in the morning?** · Evidence to consider: In 2014, the American Academy of Pediatrics recommended that middle and high schools start at 8:30 a.m. or later. · Sleep experts say teenagers need about 8–10 hours of sleep a night. · A later start could make the school day end later and affect family plans and traffic. **Card C – Should advertisements for unhealthy food be banned near schools?** · Evidence to consider: In 2023, the World Health Organization recommended strong policies to protect children from harmful food marketing. · Shops near schools depend on student customers. · Healthy foods could still be advertised. **Card D – Should every student do volunteer work during the school year?** · Evidence to consider: Saudi Vision 2030 set a goal of reaching one million volunteers a year. · Volunteering can build skills such as teamwork and leadership. · Some students already have heavy schedules."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U3-LS5-VID","script":"Phones in school? Later start times? Food ads near schools? Volunteering for everyone? Pick a side! Agree or disagree – both sides can win if the argument is strong. The bell rings for break, and half the students in the courtyard are staring at their screens… Claim. Reasons with evidence. Counterclaim and rebuttal. Call to action. Bring your essay to our class debate – and may the best argument win!"}]}],"unit_test":[{"q":"The company printed its new ___ on every bottle: *Fresh Today, Strong Tomorrow!*","answer":"slogan","explanation":"Slogan.","explanation_ar":"شعار.","options":["survey","slogan","rebuttal","bias"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"Before the vote, the student council carried out a ___ of 200 students to learn their opinions.","answer":"survey","explanation":"Survey.","explanation_ar":"استطلاع.","options":["survey","headline","commercial","rumor"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"The reporter checked every number twice to make sure her article was ___.","answer":"accurate","explanation":"Accurate.","explanation_ar":"دقيقًا.","options":["misleading","loaded","accurate","biased"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"The sound of a ___ on her phone broke her concentration during the test review.","answer":"notification","explanation":"Notification.","explanation_ar":"إشعار.","options":["claim","trial","slogan","notification"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"The class held a ___ about whether the school should have a longer lunch break.","answer":"debate","explanation":"Debate.","explanation_ar":"مناظرة.","options":["debate","headline","source","bias"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"Which phrase about a new video game uses a POSITIVE loaded word?","answer":"an unforgettable adventure","explanation":"Loaded words.","explanation_ar":"مشحونة إيجابيًا.","options":["a game with ten levels","a game for ages nine and up","an unforgettable adventure","a game that costs fifty riyals"],"dok":2,"skill":"loaded words","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"*The report seemed **credible** because it named its sources.* *Credible* means ___.","answer":"able to be trusted","explanation":"Context.","explanation_ar":"موثوق.","options":["very funny","able to be trusted","too long","full of rumors"],"dok":2,"skill":"vocabulary in context","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"Which word means *to influence someone unfairly, often without them noticing*?","answer":"manipulate","explanation":"Manipulate.","explanation_ar":"يتلاعب.","options":["communicate","express","concede","manipulate"],"dok":2,"skill":"vocabulary","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"Which sentence is an ARGUABLE claim about media lessons?","answer":"Schools should teach students how to spot fake news.","explanation":"Claim.","explanation_ar":"ادعاء قابل للنقاش.","options":["Schools should teach students how to spot fake news.","The article has six paragraphs.","Some news is shared online.","The study was published in 2018."],"dok":1,"skill":"claims","_part":"Part D – Writing skills"},{"q":"Which transition BEST introduces a rebuttal?","answer":"However,","explanation":"Transitions.","explanation_ar":"However.","options":["First,","For example,","However,","In 2018,"],"dok":1,"skill":"transitions","_part":"Part D – Writing skills"},{"q":"Which sentence EXPLAINS the evidence from the Stanford study?","answer":"This shows that students need to be taught how to recognize advertising.","explanation":"Explanation.","explanation_ar":"جملة التفسير.","options":["The study was done at Stanford.","This shows that students need to be taught how to recognize advertising.","Middle school students go to school.","Advertisements have labels."],"dok":2,"skill":"evidence","_part":"Part D – Writing skills"},{"q":"Which concession is written correctly?","answer":"It is true that students already have many subjects.","explanation":"Concession + grammar.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["It is true that students already have many subjects.","It is true, that students already has many subjects.","It true that students already have many subjects.","It is true that students already having many subjects."],"dok":2,"skill":"grammar","_part":"Part D – Writing skills"},{"q":"Which sentence about teaching media skills uses persuasive language FAIRLY?","answer":"Learning to check facts would help students make better decisions.","explanation":"Fair language.","explanation_ar":"لغة عادلة.","options":["Anyone who shares rumors is an idiot.","Fake news is destroying the entire universe.","Learning to check facts would help students make better decisions.","Everyone on Earth agrees with me."],"dok":2,"skill":"persuasive language","_part":"Part D – Writing skills"},{"q":"A student's argument essay ends with *That is what I think.* What is the BEST advice?","answer":"Restate the claim in new words and add a call to action.","explanation":"Revising.","explanation_ar":"أعد الادعاء وأضف دعوة إلى العمل.","options":["Add a new reason at the end.","Repeat the first sentence exactly.","Add a rumor to make it exciting.","Restate the claim in new words and add a call to action."],"dok":3,"skill":"conclusions","_part":"Part D – Writing skills"}],"unit_writing":{"task":"Should every school teach students how to spot fake news? Write an argument essay (200–250 words) in four paragraphs: (1) a hook and your claim; (2) two reasons, each with evidence (you may use *Check Before You Share* and *How to Spot Fake News*) and an explanation; (3) a counterclaim, a concession and a rebuttal; (4) a conclusion with a call to action.","model":"A message arrives at nine o'clock at night: school is closed tomorrow! Within minutes, forty students have shared it. By the morning, everyone discovers that it was only a rumor. Situations like this happen every day, and that is why every school should teach students how to spot fake news. (new paragraph) First, false information spreads very quickly. According to a 2018 study by researchers at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology, false stories on Twitter spread faster and reached more people than true ones. This shows that young people need checking skills before they share. Second, many students cannot tell news from advertising. In a 2016 Stanford University study, most of the middle school students tested could not recognize an advertisement labeled sponsored content. Lessons on checking sources and labels would help them. (new paragraph) Some people argue that schools already have too many subjects. It is true that timetables are full. However, media literacy does not need a new subject; teachers could include short checking activities in English, science and social studies lessons. (new paragraph) Teaching students to check before they share would protect them, their friends and their families from false information. Let's ask our school to add one fact-checking activity to every unit this year.","marks":20}});
